Innovations may be affected either by social determinism or technology determinism .
The UK Technology Education Centre states that " Social determinism refers to the hypothesis that social interactions and constructs alone determine individual behaviour ( as opposed to biological or objective factors .
Nationmasters.com states " technology determinism is a theory which points to technology as being the force which shapes society .
" Social determinisms affect demand for products and thus the revenue and profit .
Toshiba laptop is largely affected by social determinists .
Its success is determined mainly by three factors : perception of the product , economic factors and social factors .
The most important factor that has contributed to Toshiba 's success is changes in company 's perceptions towards the product .
Before the laptop was invented , the company was only selling desktop computers .
At that time , business of the company 's computers was poor and hence the investors were not willing to invest time and labour into the project .
However , the inventor , Mizoguchi , was not disheartened and worked in the dark .
After the first laptop was produced , corporate executives were still against the idea of selling it .
Fortunately , Mizoguchi 's product caught the eyes of the senior vice president of Toshiba Europe and thus he offered to sell ten thousand laptops in a year .
Even though the project took an extra two months , it helped to increase the market share of Toshiba .
This example shows that change in perception leads to different outcomes
If there had been no changes in views of investors , Toshiba laptops would not have existed in the current market .
Besides the company 's perceptions , economic also play an important role .
Resources and funding are required in an innovation to help improve it .
One is not able to come out with successful innovations by dreaming of their achievements .
Coming out with new products requires support .
For Toshiba , the profits earned from the sales of the laptops were further invested to improve the quality of the later-produced laptops .
If a product has no financial support , it will not be successful as it not able to keep up with the challenges of the newly developed technology .
The most important factor that contributes to any innovation is the response of the public .
Consumers are important groups of people who will determine the success of the product .
At that time , the offices were cramped and there was no space available for workers to place their desktops .
The newly invented laptops were able to overcome these problems because of their small size and the ability to be carried around conveniently .
This convenience efficiency of workers as they now able to work from anywhere in their offices besides their desk area .
As mentioned in the Rogers ( 2003 ) article , " By 1988 , Toshiba commanded a 38 percent market share for laptops in Europe and 21 percent in the United States ; a year later , it commanded 46 percent market share in Japan , despite the quick reactions of its competitors .
" More technology savvy people for and thus the company constantly innovated and improved in order to bring the invention to greater heights .
The Toshiba laptop was successful because of economic economic and social factors and the change in the company 's perceptions of the product .
It is not possible to succeed with only one factor .
Therefore Toshiba needs to consider all factors during their innovations .
Two types of discoveries
There were a lot of discoveries in history which resulted in the various products and technologies we use today .
Most of these discoveries were made due to normal research .
But there still existed a lot of discoveries that were made as a serendipitous result of normal research which always had some interesting stories behind them .
Although these two types of discoveries could both influence society , there are two interesting differences between them in the process .
( Rogers , 2003 )
The first difference is the purpose and the process of the research .
Usually , the normal research is due to a certain need in life and wants to make some discovery to meet the need .
All the processes in the research are to serve the purpose .
In contrast , there is always no specific need for the serendipitous discovery and the research which makes the discovery is usually for other purposes , and the serendipitous discovery is usually due to unexpected result of a normal research .
A good example is the discovery of Post-it notes .
3M Company wanted to find a kind of super glue but unexpectedly found this kind of glue that was not strong but could be used several times .
this unexpected result led to the successful production of a new kind of notes which is now the well known Post-it .
( Rogers , 2003 )
The other difference between a normal discovery and a serendipitous one is how to find the use of the discovery in the process of commercialization .
For normal discovery , this is not a big problem because the discovery is to serve a specific purpose .
So the usage of the discovery is quite clear .
But for serendipitous discovery , as the discovery is , will know the potential use of it after the discovery is made .
There is even the possibility that nobody will notice it .
So it is much harder to put it to use .
But fortunately , we had very smart and careful people who were able to find them and find a way to use them .
For example , during World War I , a British scientist called Brearly wanted to invent a kind of strong material which could be made into very good guns .
But he only invented a kind of material which had brighter luster than other materials but was not strong enough .
He was disappointed .
But later he thought why not make this beautiful material into a set ?
Finally , it turned out to be a great success .
( Accidental Inventions , 2008 )
Although there are differences between the two types of discoveries , there also some similarities between them .
For example , both were discovered in research , they to be developed to a certain degree before commercialization , and finally they to be turned into products and so on .
( Rogers , 2003 ) In conclusion , there are two main differences between normal discovery and serendipitous is the purpose and process of the research and the other one is the process to turn the discoveries into use .
Besides , there are also similarities between them .
Anyway both of them have made a great contribution to the human society and I there will be more discoveries being made , no matter which type they are .
Reference
Rogers , E. M. ( 2003 ) .
The generation of innovations .
In Diffusion of innovations ( pp.136- 158 ) .
New York : Free Press .
Brain Candy .
( 2008 ) .
Accidental Inventions .
Retrieved September 9,2009 , from http : //www.vat19.com/brain-candy/accidental-inventions-stainless-steel.cfm
Problems to Engineering Design in Singapore
Living in a small country like Singapore has various limitations is more challenging for her to compete with larger nations .
Several of these limitations significantly affect the design process of new innovations .
A majority of these limitations would increase the cost of the innovation and hence require careful design .
One of these problems includes the city-state 's lack of land space which would affect the structural design of the innovation particularly if it was on a large scale .
Another would be Singapore 's dense population that affects the location and structural considerations of the design process .
Singapore currently has a land space 683 square kilometers , a country barely visible on the map .
A strategy adopted by the government to solve this problem is land reclamation , which currently accounts for about 10 % of its land space .
To further maximize land space , Singapore 's structures are mainly skyscrapers with strong and often costly foundations .
This issue particularly affects innovations that are on a larger scale , as large costs will be incurred in the construction of their foundations .
This increase in cost will hence affect the market value and affect the economic analysis stage of the design process .
Take for example the housing in Singapore , which comprises mainly Housing Development Board [ HDB ] flats .
In view of the collapse of Nicoll highway back in April 2004 , HDB has put up very strict requirements for the piling process and the materials used , like grade 30 concrete should be used in piling .
Similarly , the steel structure that forms the backbone of the buildings require a minimum of 3mm steel gauge .
All these may drive up cost and hence affect the design process .
Aside from the problem of the lack of land space , there is also the consideration of its extremely dense population .
Contrary to its limited land area as discussed earlier , it houses more than 4 million people .
During the peak hours , crowded trains and bus stations will be hard to miss .
innovations thus have to consider its design rather seriously given the circumstances .
All these will seriously affect the functionality analysis stage of the design process , as more time and consideration will have to be channeled into this stage .
One example of a properly located and well-designed amenity would be the Mass Rail Transit [ MRT ] stations .
In addition , some of these stations like City Hall and Dhoby Ghaut station , not only play the role of an important hub in Singapore 's transport system , but also an internal shopping center within itself .
City- link , which is connected to city hall station , is an underground network of shops that is linked to other larger shopping facilities .
Such an example shows how proper planning of the amenities to the success of the application .
In conclusion , being a small country , Singapore has several issues that may affect the design process of future innovations , particularly its limited land space and its extremely dense population .
Certain examples like the MRT and the HDB flats are evidence that even with limited resources , Singapore can still flourish .
With proper design and careful planning , perhaps these limitations would be a thing of the past .
