Compare and contrast about guncotton and explosives
In most people 's opinion technology can only developed or created via research .
Considering new explosives , in order to find new explosives ,Nobel spent nearly all his time doing experiments in his library .
However , some technology was invented from a serendipitous discovery .
Guncotton was invented by Schoenbein by chance .
The difference between these two inventions is the ideas about how to invent them emerged in different ways .
Nobel a very hardworking and brave man .
He used nearly all his time to work for new explosive and his younger brother died in a failed experiment .At the same time his brother 's death had an unbalancing effect on his father .
His father died right after his younger brother ( Nils .
R , N.d ) .
But he never gave up and risked his life to go on his invention and his ideas came from his effort .
The story about how Schoenbein invented guncotton is quite an interesting story .
According to Yang ( 2007 ) , " when Schoenbein was doing experiments , he overthrew a bottle of chemical solution , and the solution flowed on the table . Schoenbein found nothing but his wife 's apron to clean the table .
After he had finished cleaning he thought that his wife will blame him after she saw what he had done .
So he wanted to use the stove to dry the apron .
When he walked near the stove the apron burned without any smoke and left ashes .
Then he felt very happy because he realized that he had found a new kind of explosive .
" Nobel could invent the explosive successfully because of his diligence and the attitude towards science which made him never give up .
Schoenbein came up with his idea accidentally and finally made a great invention for the word .
There are also some similarities between these two technologies .
First of all they are both invented by prudent chemists .
If Noble was not a prudent person and did not do his experiments and observe the phenomenon though he is hardworking he may never have invented the new explosive successfully .
From the story of how Schoenbein came up with the idea about the guncotton we can tell that he is really a lucky scientist .
But without the prudential attitude towards science he may have missed his good luck and did not go on finding how can this phenomenon occur .
Secondly these two technologies both made great contributions to the industry and military affairs .
As both of them are explosives people use them to exploit mines , tear down great buildings and provide a high temperature for many chemical experiments .
They are also widely used in battlefield .
Engineers use them to make all kinds of weapons .
Technology , whether it was developed via research or resulted from a serendipitous experiment scientist 's greatest contribution to the world
Reference
Yang , M.M. ( 2007,July ) .Shaonianyuekan , from { http : //www.cnki.com.cn/Article/CJFDTotal-YKSN200713023.htm } [ HYPERLINK : http : //www.cnki.com.cn/Article/CJFDTotal-YKSN200713023.htm ]
The 'Made in China ' phenomenon has signaled China 's endeavor to catch up with the advanced western countries .
However , the unprecedentedly rapid economic growth , driven by manufacturing and export industry , probably prevents people from discovering the problems that may impede engineering design process for innovation .
Two of these problems involve the special political environment and the Chinese people 's mindset , which already have negative influence on several stages of engineering design process for innovation in China .
First of all , the special political environment many account for the stalling of some stages of the whole process , such as the stage of recognizing a need or problem and the stage of innovation-development .
In fact , if we date back to the earliest related term in the party 's document , as it turns out , " it is not until October 2000 and the Fifth Session of the Fifteenth Party Congress that the 'cultural industries ' were formally proposed as part of the recommendations document for the national Tenth Five-Year Plan " ( Zhang Xiaoming , Hu Hulin and Zhang Jiangang , 2004 , as cited in Keane , 2007 , p.81 ) .
On the other hand , for a system so guided by the central planning , having such decision makers means that it probably will have difficulty recognizing a need or a problem .
Consequently , research and development ( R&D ) activities are not likely to be stimulated instantaneously .
Even though R&D activities are somehow initiated successfully , the nature of central planning which calls for " information to be moved up and down the hierarchy " ( Chan , 2009 ) may throw obstacles in the information-exchange process , which is significant to the innovation-development process .
Additionally , as Keane ( 2007 ) cited in his book 'Created in China ' , " socialism overplayed the role of productive forces and failed to develop the innovative potential of human capital " .
For instance , Gage ( 2006 ) showed that in a case study published by the Washington Policy Centre , a non-partisan think tank , health facilities were required to get permission from the State Health Agency to build and extend their health care facilities .
As unveiled in the research report , it somehow took longer for the health facilities to obtain approval from the State Health Agency than to actually build or extend the needed facilities .
In truth , the hospital would not be allowed to renovate unless it discarded ten beds
As a result , the central planning actually created monopolies in this case , and the communication between manufacturing and supplier broke down due to such central planning interference .
Thus , the demands of consumers and the free market could not be met .
Another problem refers to Chinese people 's mindset .
As Michael Keane ( 2007 ) has noted , the common assumption existing long among the Chinese was that productivity directly equals material production .
" In other word , a focus on production and performance indicators in China had impacted negatively on innovation " .
Therefore , even today in China , it is still reasonable to argue that the process for innovation may not be well understood , which means , in the respective of knowledge degree of technology transfer , the innovation-development does not seem capable to function well .
Moreover , according to what E. M. Rogers ( 2003 ) stated in 'The Generation of Innovation ' , in the face of high degree of uncertainty , these social factors in innovation-development process determine what technology is actually available to the consumers .
To sum up , we are all witnessing the state 's transformation from 'Made in China ' to 'Created in China' .
In the current stage , political environment and people 's mindset are two of the problems that may hamper engineering design process for innovation .
Furthermore , these problems already have impact on several stages of the process .
