Scientific American .
P 88-97 .
Genetic testing is a kind of test which determines the vulnerabilities of an individual to inherit genetic disorders ( U.S National Library of Medicine , 2009 , para .
1 ) .
The results obtained from a genetic test can exclude an alleged genetic condition or help verify if a person is a carrier of a genetic disease or have a chance to develop a genetic disorder in future ( U.S National Library of Medicine , 2009 , para .
1 ) .
Upon testing , the individual requesting genetic testing should not have the final say in whether to reveal the results of such testing ( Norrgard , 2008 , Reproductive and Other Familial Concerns , para .
2 ) .
Family members of the individual requesting genetic testing should also have the rights to know about the results of such testing .
This is because that they might have a higher risk for developing this genetic disease or are already a carrier of this mutation .
Only by recognizing this possibility earlier , they can then make informed choices about their family planning decisions , career and other life choices ( Norrgard , 2008 , Genetic Testing is a Matter of Individual Choice , para .
1 ) .
The use of in vitro fertilization ( IVF ) method can be used for family planning .
By using this method , only embryos which are free from any genetic disorders are transferred back to women 's uterus and allowed to develop to full birth ( Norrgard , 2008 , Family Planning , para .
2 ) .
Alternatively , they can choose chorionic villus sampling ( CVS ) , a form of genetic prenatal testing ( Norrgard , 2008 , Family Planning , para .
2 ) .
If the foetus is diagnosed with the mutation , the couple can then choose to terminate the pregnancy ( Norrgard , 2008 , Family Planning , para .
2 ) .
With advances in technology , many genetic diseases which were once considered untreatable might be cured in the near future ( Allen , 2006 , Future samples , para .
7 ) .
It is therefore important to provide close relatives who might be at risk a chance to test whether they are affected by the genetic disease .
This will help them determine their course of treatment or at least make precautions ( Allen , 2006 , Future samples , para .
7 ) .
Furthermore , it is also unfair to the insurance companies if the individual with a genetic disease decides to buy life insurance policies without revealing that they have such genetic disorder .
The insurance companies need to be cost effective too .
They should therefore have the rights to request for a medical record ( Hsien , 2007 , The question : Should genetic information be disclosed to insurers ? , para .
2 ) .
In conclusion , the individual requesting genetic testing has the obligation to inform others of his condition ( Norrgard , 2008 , Reproductive and Other Familial Concerns , para .
2 ) .
