( 395 words w/o citations )
Rapid advances in genetic research and technology led to important advances in the detection of genetic conditions and diseases ( Chris and Bryn 2002 ) .
Genetic testing has , however , raised ethical concerns like whether the individual requesting genetic testing should have the final say on whether to reveal the results of such testing ( Chris and Bryn 2002 ) .
There are severe consequences if the results are not handled and communicated properly by third parties .
Therefore , individuals should have the final say .
Individuals are to be treated equally as required by law unless there are relevant reasons like differences in skill , knowledge or experience ( Chris and Bryn 2002 ) .
However , it is very likely that employers will use the results to discriminate against employeess based on unfounded perceptions of health risks and possible future disabilities ( Chris and Bryn 2002 ) ( Neil and Ronald 2006 ) .
Likewise , insurance companies may make use of the results to charge a higher premium or not provide insurance because of possible chances of high medical bills in the future ( Chris and Bryn 2002 ) ( Neil and Ronald 2006 ) .
Doctors have to adhere to medical ethics , namely , they are required to respect the confidentiality of the patients ( Thirumoorthy 2009 ) .
Both the Hippocratic Oath and International Code of Ethics states that without the prior consent from the patient doctors are not allowed to disclose any information which he has obtained ( Thirumoorthy 2009 ) .
Hence , by revealing the genetic testing result without permission from the individual , it constitutes a breach of medical confidentiality .
Even to family members , individuals should have the final say .
Like the phrase , " Ignorance is bliss " , if the results are not related to the family members or they have lower risk of getting the genetic disorder , telling them may cause unnecessary worries ( Agatha , Denise and Kathleen 2009 ) ( Gillian , Denise and Bartha 2009 ) .
There are also considerations like age and maturity ( Gillian , Denise and Bartha 2009 ) .
If the family member is old and no longer under high risk or a child who is too young to be at risk , there is no need to reveal disturbing results that may cause potential psychological harm to them ( Agatha , Denise and Kathleen 2009 ) ( Gillian , Denise and Bartha 2009 ) .
Some individuals may also be waiting for the " right time " , such as avoiding the need to disrupt or jeopardize major life events like graduation , marriage , pregnancy , childbirth ( Gillian , Denise and Bartha 2009 ) .
In addition , the individual might not even be close to their family members .
In fact , there may even be bad blood between them .
The family members may instead use the results against the individual .
In conclusion , the individual requesting genetic testing should have the final say on whether to reveal the results of genetic testing .
