The use of surveillance technology such as RFID to track people should be banned
For decades , people have been debating whether the use of surveillance technology should be bbanned . They.. .
privacy ( Stanley...2003 ) . However , imagine what would happen if we shut down all the surveillance technology in thcountry . Banks will.. .
The purpose of using surveillance technology is to maintain social stability and to protect us and our assets . Hence , ... should not be banned .
RFID , security cameras , Global Positioning Systems ( GPS ) on mobile phone and some eavesdropping device are some of the common surveillance gadget used by the FBI and police .
In the 20th century , the best weapons against terrorism are no longer bullets and bombs because the focus is to prevent criminal activities before it happenhappens . Hence , ...nformation is the key .
( Piller & Lichtblau , July29 2002 )
In the U.S. , FBI and police used surveillance technology to track suspect and to fight terrorism .
" Calls made from a mobile phone led Italian police to one of the suspects in the failed suicide bombings in London on 21 July .
" ( BBC news , Aug3 2005 ) In this incident , the police caught the criminal by tapping his mobile phone , listening to his conversation and use his mobile phone signal to determine his location .
This is a classic example of how surveillance technology can be used to fight crime .
Surveillance technology can also be used in the medical fields .
Presbyterian Hospital in Charlotte , North Carolina came out with a RFID infant protection system where a RFID chips was tagged onto the infant .
( Two Stories Highlight the RFID Debate , July19 2005 ) The security system will sound off if the infant was being illegally removed from the nursery .
This system successfully recovered a kidnapped infant by using RFID signal on the tagged baby to track its location .
GPS chip was also placed on Alzheimer 's patient shoes to track their whereabouts .
A patient with this disease can be easily disoriented , hence , causing them to lose their way .
( Wong , Jun10 2009 ) The use of surveillance technology can help the patient 's family members to locate him/ her and bring him/ her home safely .
Although the use of surveillance technology will raise ethical concerns , to obtain important information that might save thousands of lives , there is no choice but to do it .
By tracking a suspect 's whereabouts or on his or her conversation , we might be invading on his or her privaprivacy . However , ... if we can prevent another September 11 incident from re- occurring , I believe everyone would agree to it .
From just the above example , we can see that the advantages of using surveillance technology overweigh its disadvantages .
In conclusion , we can impose a law and limit the use of surveillance technology but we can not ban it .
