Topic2 : " The individual requesting genetic testing should have the final say in whether to reveal the results of such testing .
"
Genetic testing has evolved and is now more prevalent in our modern society .
In light of this ubiquitous and accessible availability of genetic testing , many have started to question the problems that are associated with the results .
The question now is whether an individual should decide whether to release the results to others or that the results be revealed regardless of his approval .
There is evidence that the latter proved more beneficial to both the patient and others around him .
Many have argued that by revealing genetic results , companies will be able to gain access to them and thus deny those affected from being hired due to their disabilities .
However , it is important for many companies to know these disabilities because these may pose problems for certain operations .
For example , people with sickle cell trait are not able to be pilots because they may face problems at high altitude .
( Why might genetic information be relevant to employment ? , n.d. , para.1 ) Furthermore , industries that handle certain chemicals or carcinogens should have stringent employability criteria .
This does not only protect the companies ' interest but also protect hopeful employees from aggravating their conditions .
( Why might genetic information be relevant to employment ? , n.d. , para.2 ) .
In sum , revealing genetic results will be beneficial to the patients as well as companies involved in the recruitment process .
Accessible genetic results are also important to patients and their families .
Patients with positive test results will be susceptible to physical and emotional stress and thus will need support from theiroved ones .
( Sharing Genetic Test Results With Your Family , 2009 , para .
2 ) .
Moreover , the results may also shed some light to certain predominant problems that the entire family may be susceptible to in the near future .
This will allow relatives to start early treatment before the disease develops .
( Sharing Genetic Test Results With Your Family , 2009 , para .
7 ) .
Therefore , availability of genetic information will help ease the pressure on the patient and also allow many to go through early treatment or take preventive measures .
Albeit many believe in human rights as well as the protection of one 's interest , there are many other prevailing factors that tip the balance toward releasing results and making it readily available for the good of the patient and his surrounding people .
