" The individual requesting genetic testing should have the final say in whether to reveal the results of such testing .
"
Over the years , drastic advancement in genetic testing allows the detection of defective genes associated with many hereditary diseases .
This impact of genetic testing posed on the individual and their family has brought up many debates on the ethical , social and family issues .
Some suggest that revealing of genetic results could benefit family members as they could seek appropriate medical treatment if tested positive
( Jacobs & Deatrick 1999 . However , negative implications such as violation of privacy , long- termed psychological and sociological effects inflicted on the , outweigh the . Therefore individuals should have the final say in whether to reveal their genetic results or not .
Firstly , an individual should not be robbed of his basic right to privacy .
Although , it can not be denied that it is ethical to inform family members about their risk of hereditary , one does not have any obligation to reveal their genetic status since this information could be exploited and misused by others thus threatening their welfare
( Jacobs & Deatrick 1999 ) .
Thus , they should have the right to decide either to disclose their genetic information or not and also to what extent they want to publicize it .
Secondly , revelation of one 's genetic status subjects the individual to many social issues related to genetic discrimination
( Bereano , 2000 . For instance , insurance companies would reduce healthcare coverage , increase health premiums or terminate the health insurance policies for individuals with potential genetic risk
( Bereano , 2000 . Furthermore , discrimination imposed by employers and colleagues could influence an individual 's self- esteem and psychological well-being .
Lastly , family dilemma could arise due to the tension caused by the unfavorable psychological influences of genetic testing
( Doukas & Berg , 2001 ) .
Since every individual has a different level of acceptance , disclosure of genetic results could cause them to experience unnecessary emotional distress
( Doukas & Berg , 2001 ) .
Hence , an individual should decide rationally whether to disclose their genetic results if it inflicts harm and misery to their family .
In conclusion , individuals should have the privacy to determine whether to reveal their genetic results or not since it not only inflicts emotional distress on their family but also subjects them to social discrimination .
