Allocation of the state 's budget has to be justifiable .
With competing interests that necessitate public spending , public spending on the aged would most likely be restricted .
Our government needs to be pragmatic in its allocation of financial resources .
several areas of country 's developments such as expanding on economic capability through business investment , allocating financial resources on infrastructure and technology , and investing on human capital seem to be of importance than providing to the financial needs of the aged .
In fact , a more prosperous economy should provide the economic means to support the public spending on the aged .
Thus , managing the economy should come first .
A pragmatic government would rather channel resources to other areas of the country 's singular .
Therefore , public spending on the aged should and will be limited .
Economic growth is the basis to substantiate the monetary and social welfare benefits for the aged people .
In most countries , a large fiscal budget is on the engines of economic growth .
In Singapore , the engines of growth are from the tourism sector and knowledge and research intensive industries .
In the tourism sector , the focus is on the two integrated resorts which are currently being developed .
It is critical for such investments to establish as it provides employment opportunities .
Increase in tourism arrivals and the attraction of high end consumers will result in generation of high tourism revenues and revenues from high end retail shops .
On the other hand , knowledge and capital intensive industries have started up quite recently in Singapore .
Fusionpolis , Biopolis and business hubs have been set up in various parts of Singapore .
With all these economic developments , our economy can be expanded and sustained .
A stronger economy leads to higher household income .
Consequently , a larger tax base should then sufficiently provide a larger portion of public finance for the aged .
History reminds us of the contributions by our forefathers .
It is thus the responsibility of the society to look after them .
In countries such as the United Kingdom Kingdom , welfare systems and pension schemes are in place .
In contrast , public spending for the aged is limited in Singapore .
In fact , investment should be on nurturing of our people as they are contributors to the economy .
training , re-training and investing on quality education , nurture the people .
Educational institutions provide quality teaching while re-training centres impart new skills so that skills do not become obsolete .
More public finance on the aged will compromise the investments on human capital .
In addition , it only satisfies the social and moral obligations to the aged .
Being pragmatic , we should look towards creating a future for ourselves and the future generation .
In addition , the aged are not the only ones who require monetary support , other needy groups , such as the handicaps , also require monetary support to fund their healthcare needs .
Therefore , the emphasis is not just on the aged .
Nonetheless , the aged will also benefit monetarily when the economy prospers and tax payment is increased .
Most countries have embraced the wonders of technology .
Like the rest of the countries , Singapore has infused huge financial resources on investment for technological upgrades and infrastructural developments .
The installation of an islandwide wireless broadband service is one of the recent technological upgrades .
With this service provision , it enables everyone to stay connected .
Apart from the improvement in telecommunication , our transport network has recently been extended , with an addition of the Circle Line route , which will lead to an increase to the total number of MRT stations by 2020 .
An improved public transport system increases accessiblity and reduces travelling time for most people .
Although these projects have huge costs borne by the government , the benefits still outweigh the cost .
Society benefits as these developments can be accessed by the public .
In addition , both of these long term provisions will reap in profits .
In comparison to public funds on aged , government only serves the social responsibility of looking after the aged .
In conclusion , aged people can not rely on the government to take full responsibility for their needs since there are other priorities in the country 's developments which will create job opportunities , greater economic revenues , convenience and efficiency.Though the government has a social responsibility to look after the aged , families should take up the bulk of responsibility for caring and providing essential needs .
With more competing interests for financial resources , public finance on the aged is of least concern .
it will definitely put a strain on the economic budget and does not reap any return if public spending on the aged has no limits .
In many countries , there is a common phase , a baby-boomer period .
Now , another phase that most nations are facing is the ageing population .
Countries are aware that this issue is pervasive and it will have profound implications to the facets of the society if they are not responding fast to it .
However , the question is , how much of a focus should a country place in it when there are other important areas to look into as well ?
With the stiff competition amongst nations , public spending on the aged should be spent only if necessary , but more focus should be placed in other aspects like for example , education , tourism and security which are the key factors to a country 's economy and thus the survival of it .
Funds placed in these aspects can help ease the ageing process in the long run , critically looking at minimizing the dependency of the aged on the governments .
I believe that measures to ease ageing issues can be done at a low cost and can be at the same time .
There are several viable methods such as
Strong emphasis of family support and creating an awareness about taking responsibility filial obligation , insurance , healthy lifestyle , financial planning and ensuring the aged have enough retirement fund .
There are more private sectors dealing in health and nutrition and financial consultants to help in the management of assets .
More support could be given to all these sectors from the government .
It is more of a one time method , when inculcated to become a habit and trend .
Information may not reach out to the uneducated . Hence funds have to be allocated to help them .
These are necessary expenditure that is unavoidable .
Some argue , that the aged are also part of a community and that we should make sure we can provide more for them as well .
It is more ethical to do that and take up the responsibilities rather than finding them a social burden and to make sure that there are enough " funds for them or even to neglect them because of pragmatic reasons .
As much as all nations want this , for countries with limited resources , a choice has to be made based on practicality .
With more funding allocated to other development , more social issues can be resolved , allowing more potential economic growth .
It sounds unfair , but it is definitely a solution that a nation should employ .
It is inevitable .
With allocation of funds more to other development , it will ensure that we need not make such heartless and controversial decision in the future .
plural can encourage voluntary welfare organization to help the aged aged .
Responsibilities will be spread among larger groups .
Last of all , placing more funds to enhance and maintain the security and economy of a nation should be one of the top priorities .
Without this , a nation will face stronger challenges . Helping the aged will then not even be possible .
It has been proven that countries with strong economy and security do pose a threat to others . A good example are the four giants .
in the context of a small country such as Singapore , besides taking a diplomatic approach , the country also has to make sure that her economy is strong enough for foreign companies to invest in .
This ensures her safety unlike countries like Afghanistan and Pakistan .
When more jobs are created , business prospers more and more could then be done to support the aged .
Although , this strategy may lessen the attractiveness for foreigners to choose the country for retirement due to less policies and emphasis on the aged , it definitely helps a country to a larger extent .
Countries without good economy have more social issues like corruption , crime rates etc. Countries with poor security , military force , usually ca n't really negotiate terms .
With strong economy and security , it ensure a nation 's long-term status , which covers the welfare of the nation , indirectly making sure that the aged still has people to support .
The aged should not be forgotten but spending on them needs to be limited and only if needed .
However , this will require the whole community 's effort to make sure that cultivation of moral values and virtues are deeply rooted in them .
This is to ensure that the future batch of elderly are well-prepared for their own ageing process and not just wait for the government to take care of them .
Public spending on aged can then be reduced much more and more must be placed in other developments that can bring prosperity to the nation and minimize social issues .
With much credit to swift medical advancements , the average life span of a human being in America has shot up by 5 years in just a decade to 82 years today .
Such increase in longevity and the reduce in birth rates , will no doubt lead to an inevitable rise to the problems of aging , as there are fewer and fewer young people to support the rising number of elderly , both financially and/ socially .
Aging is a big issue , governments from all over the world are pushing forward policies and Research and Developments to stem its economical and social impacts on a country .
Aging is one process most people dread , especially for those without children .
In Singapore , our government has recently implemented a policy known as Lift Improvement and Facilities Enhancement , which aims to improve the living conditions of single elderly or those who have been abandoned by their families .
Residential areas with high elderly density are set to undergo upgrading to provide more elderly-friendly-facilities .
Installation of lifts on every level and hand bars in the toilets , for instance , can help elderly overcome certain movement difficulties which they encounter in their daily lives .
The setting up of the senior citizens ' corner can also act as a platform for interaction among the elderly , especially when travelling out of their house is made easier . They would be more willing to engage in more social activities , thus becoming more receptive to happenings around them and learn to be more self reliant .
Also , the new alarm system and window grills can also ensure a higher level of security in their area .
This current policy of facility upgrades is very comprehensive as it ensures convenience , security , entertainment and thus the social well-being of elderly .
One of our current policies to ease the aging process is by encouraging the inclusion of elderly in our workforce .
Lowered barriers of entry to the elder employment , allows the elderly to enter the workforce and maintain a spot in the society .
With the elderly working , they can gain an additional source of income for their family and increase the productivity of the society .
However , it is often argued that such policies could actually stifle competition between the current working group and the elderly .
Working adults , who are already putting too much focus on their career rather than on family planning , tend to postpone their child bearing plans even further .
In such a situation , there will be a surge in productivity initially , but we may expect a serious drop in the long run which aggravates the aging problem due to the shrinking population .
But in the case of elderly employment in Singapore , they tend to be given menial jobs , due to their physical and mental handicap as compared to younger workers . these menial jobs are unattractive to the career-driven adults in Singapore are filled up by these older workers .
According to the Ministry of Manpower , such jobs are usually occupied by people aged 65 and above and there is still a prevailing shortage of manpower .
Therefore , it would be desirable to have the elderly join the workforce as they could serve to aid in the economy with their spending power and even reduce their need for social welfare , which can be highly taxing to an economy .
The number one killer of elderly is heart failure .
For instance , in- text citation is required , 5 % of all the people aged 65 and above suffers from heart failure .
Although some elderly who suffer gentle heart failure could still maintain a relatively normal life , those who suffer to a severe extent have their day-to-day activities affected .
They no longer have the ability to pursue a normal and perhaps happy life .
However with R&D in the area of heart-related diseases , has led to the developments of new drugs and treatments to control and reduce the effects of heart failures in their everyday life .
Hence our current R&Ds have no doubt helped elderly by increasing their quality of life .
All in all , aging is part of the natural life cycle of an organism .
Everyone has a role to play to solve the persisting aging problem in many developed societies .
Policies to upgrade housing in Singapore have improved the living conditions of the elderly , together with the setting up of the senior citizens ' corners I am convinced that the elderly can now participate more actively in the society and live off with a higher social well being .
As for the improvements in current R&D , we can all anticipate more ground-breaking inventions to relief elderly of pains from illness .
Also , having the elderly joining the workforce can increase the total productivity and reduce the burden piled on family members .
Nonetheless , I am convinced that current research and developments policies do play an important role to help ease the arduous aging process .
In this ever competitive world , the idea of the weak being more susceptible to elimination and the strong emerging victorious is not unfounded .
Indeed , this concept is applicable to a real life context in our society - the old versus the young workers .
The society now has this notion that the old would eventually become obsolete , exhausted and unproductive .
However , the aged has come a long way before they hit the supposed end of their economic lifespan .
Without their contributions , the modern society might not even exist .
The state has a certain responsibility to provide for the old .
To simply cut back funds on the grounds that the elderly can no longer yield any economic profits is an irresponsible act .
The old are worthy of every resources spent on them as they are still important assets to the nation .
In Singapore , much emphasis has been placed on providing a holistic education .
Children are spoilt for choices when it comes to choosing their desired education route and with a fourth university in the planning , learning has never been easier .
Nevertheless , no matter how refined the education system is , it could never instil moral values in children which are critical in tackling the ageing problem .
The State only has a partial duty in alleviating the situation of the elderly ; the rest would belong to their closest kin .
Changing norms and mindsets coupled with an increasingly high standard of living , is driving children to abandon their parents .
With no one to rely on or any proper financial aid , the elderly would be unable to fend for themselves and perhaps left homeless .
This would result in a degrading of the society as the wandering aged would eventually resort to desperate measures such as begging , stealing and possibly dying on the streets .
The increase in life expectancy would indicate a growing pool of aged workers .
As this segment of the population becomes more significant , spending is unavoidable .
In fact , the resources allocated do not have to be solely for the old as other sectors could also seek betterment from it .
For instance , the Lift Upgrading Programme ( LUP ) benefits the old as well as the disabled and the general citizens .
The society must avoid the pitfalls of classifying and relating old age to low productivity .
Ageing does not equate to redundancy .
The elderly have had accumulated a considerable amount of refined expertise that are still of societal use .
Instead of seeing them as a sector that would yield no returns , the old should be considered as an investment where providing them opportunities to share their experiences would enable the young to gain precious knowledge and learn from past mistakes .
This would pave the way for a more meaningful future for the subsequent ageing generation .
For countries with a small population such as that of Singapore , there exists the need to recruit foreign talents to fuel the economy .
Reduced tax rates , solid infrastructure and business-tailored policies are some of strategies utilized by the local government .
However , this would not sustain the interest in foreigners if the country lacks an appealing living condition .
One way to retain these foreigners is to improve the welfare system for the aged .
This is not a totally farfetched approach based on the previous case where Singapore established a comprehensive child care system that resulted in many expatriates moving their family over to work and dwell here .
We could similarly use an impeccable retirement scheme to entice them to settle down permanently .
By making Singapore a better place for old age , it could resolve the issue of a diminutive pool of human resources as well as boost the State 's economy .
All in all , the elderly should not be viewed as incapable of providing any further services to the nation .
Rather than condemning their low productivity , we should incorporate the old into our community for they had garnered experiences which are of practical use to the young .
The state shares a part in supporting the elderly for these are the people who had a hand in nation building and morphing the society which we live in now .
Spending on the old does not denote an economic dead end .
To the contrary , the old has the potential to open new doors and bring about more opportunities both within and into the country .
Taking care of the aged is now a global issue that no country can steer clear of .
Some people might think that we should take a pragmatic approach and ignore the needs of the elderly as they can no longer serve the society .
However , who can deny that our older generations have contributed immensely to our society ?
No one can also be sure that all the aged people can lead good lives without any federal help .
Therefore , whether it is due to moral obligation or for fulfilling one 's responsibility , compromising public spending on other areas of the country 's development for the aged is unavoidable .
It is true that by doing so , a country 's development and productivity will suffer .
Nevertheless , I believe that as long as the government is able to obtain a balance in the areas of public spending , the country 's development can still progress without limiting public spending on the aged .
It is only right for us to take care of the aged as they contributed a great deal to what we have now .
Without the hard work of the older generation , we would not have the fundamentals of our society .
We can only prosper with a strong foundation , which the elderly had given us .
As a result , when they become feeble and require assistance , we should lend them a helping hand .
Not to mention , it is also a moral value to take care of the elderly .
However , spending money on them could have disadvantaged other groups of needy people .
For instance , it could have deprived the children in low income families from education .
Therefore , there is a need to organize strong branches of organizations , like Community Development Councils ( CDCs ) , to decide who to help in extreme cases .
The government should also engage in long term solutions , like Research and Development , to save money .
This would ensure the welfare fund is being distributed evenly .
To add on , less spending on the aged could have caused social problems .
Cases of conflicts over money problems at home ; elderly loitering in void decks and parks ; lonely elderly found dead and only discovered after a few days , are not unheard of .
Reducing funds on this growing age group could cause these problems as the attention paid towards them would then be cut .
Some mentioned that it should be the children 's responsibility to take care of elderly and some of the problems are inevitable .
Nonetheless , government intervention can minimize the possibility of having these problems .
The Maintenance of Parents Act is one good example of government intervention where taking care of elderly is .
Under CPF Hosing Grant , there is now an incentive to live near parents too .
Thus , there is more likelihood for the younger generation to take care of their parents .
If the government can put in enough funds for the elderly and handle the aging problem holistically , this would benefit the country in the long run .
Working class Singaporeans would be motivated to work hard as they know the government would contribute in taking care of them as they aged .
This could stimulate economic growth .
Foreign talents would also be attracted to migrate here when we establish a welfare state image .
There is a worry that the diversion of spending could also result in a country 's slow growth and low productivity .
Nevertheless , countries with more limited expenditure do no better or worse economically than countries with high social expenditure .
Taking Denmark for example , it is a welfare state , but yet , it is one of the top countries in income ranking ( OECD , full stop after in-text citation It is also important to implement measures that require people to rely on oneself rather than the government .
On the whole , a country 's public spending requires very delicate planning .
Since the aging population is one big issue now , necessary spending on the aged can not be stingily kept away .
Not only is it due to moral obligation , ignoring the aging population would also result in other social problems that might be even more difficult to solve .
As the government wants to maintain a country 's development , public spending on the aged should be limited but not to the minimal .
It should be at the maximum limit where the government can still withstand so as to help as many elderly as possible .
A nation 's success does not solely depend on economic growth but the welfare aspect too .
Therefore with careful planning of expenditure , solving aging problem generously would benefit the country in long run .
