56 , No .
1 ( March 1982 ) , pg .
85-107
The rapidly aging population has caused a dilemma for the government .
Revenue from taxes is insufficient for the government to develop the country .
The increasing dependency of the elderly is causing a burden to the national budget .
" Development is becoming a people-centered process , whose ultimate goal must be the improvement of the human condition .
" commented by Boutros Boutros-Ghali ( sixth Secretary-General of the United Nations ) .
Without development , there will be no progress , whether economic , social , or political .
If governments neglect the importance of a country 's development and choose to spend more on the elderly instead , it will get us nowhere .
Public spending on the elderly should be limited , so money can be better spent on the country 's development , such as technological advancements for the betterment of the nation .
It is widely believed that the government should care for the elderly , in return for their valuable contributions to the country .
However , the country 's modernization should be prioritized , as this helps to improve the welfare of the elderly in the long run .
In most developing countries , the budget deficit is one of the important indicators of the country 's economic prowess .
When expenditure exceeds income due to a rising aging population , certain expenses have to be reduced .
This includes limiting public spending on the elderly .
Maintaining or increasing the budget allocations for the elderly regardless of limited resources means running the risk of larger budget deficits .
Focusing on the country 's development is a long term measure to double government coffers .
An increase of income can be used for financial aid for the elderly .
This is proven during a recession , where financial packages for the elderly are temporarily reduced .
The elderly usually form a minority of the population in a society .
In Singapore , the elderly comprises of only 8.7 % out of the total population .
So besides concern for the elderly , the welfare of society has to be taken care of .
The government should increase spending in public amenities and infrastructure .
Every government must fulfill their duty to build a society , where all citizens have equal rights , and not just focus on the elderly .
Education for the youth should be stressed more , school and other facilities ought to be built .
We should invest more on the young and adults instead of the elderly , as it is more promising to do so .
Thanks to government allocations in education , healthcare , and infrastructure , the society and economy can grow .
This is only possible with public spending in sectors other than financial aid for the aged .
Every government should see it as their responsibility to create and sustain a society where their citizens , in particular the youth , can secure a livelihood and build a future ( Thalif Deen , 2006 ) .
It is important for the government to know their role , to provide opportunity for their citizens to grow .
Lack of development will render a country rudderless in the sea of a more modern and competitive world .
''Migrants should be able to migrate out of choice rather than out of necessity,' ' says the 25-member European Union .
It seems that workers tend to move out from an undeveloped country to seek better job opportunities
And where do they go ?
To the well developed countries of course .
Of the 191 million international migrants last year , about 115 million lived in developed countries , according to UN figures ( Thalif Deen ) .
This shows that becoming a developed country not only prevents the lost of workforce but also attracts talents to join our nation .
This in turn increases income tax and corporate tax collection which can be used for the benefit of the elderly .
A larger and efficient workforce will be good news for the civil service and private sector .
This is only possible with public spending in sectors other than financial aid for the aged .
Obviously , the notion of reimbursing the elderly with financial aid for their contributions to the country is naive .
There are plenty of methods that can provide aid the elderly .
Elder dependency should be reduced by encouraging later retirement ( Peter Peterson , 2001 ) .
This is an example of alternatives that can be used to reduce spending on the elderly .
So reducing spending on the elderly for the sake of a country 's development is a wise act .
Reference list entry : Peter G. Peterson ( 2001 ) .
A Graying World .
Harvard International Review .
Thelif Deen ( 2006 ) .
DEVELOPMENT : Pros and Cons of International Migration .
Electronic References .
Retrieved September 14 , 2009 , from { http : //ipsnews.net/news.asp ? idnews = 34748 } [ HYPERLINK : http : //ipsnews.net/news.asp ? idnews = 34748 ]
While public spending on the aged is vital , it should not comprise other aspects of a country 's growth and development .
As mentioned by Friedland and Summer ( 2005 ) , " With little economic growth , society faces fewer choices on how to care for those who are least able to care for themselves .
On the other hand , with sufficient economic growth , there are more choices and fewer persons in need " .
This underlines the importance of economic development in the country to neutralize the implications of an aging population .
Thus , instead of spending excessively on the aged , resources of the country should be diverted to other areas like healthcare , education , and infrastructure , which will in turn benefit the country economically .
Despite the above , it may be argued that as the elderly contributed significantly to the country 's development in their younger days , in view of their long term hard work , they should be entitled to more benefits than the rest of the population .
Governments should also set aside sufficient funds to assist them in coping with their old age life , be it in terms of financial or social assistance .
For instance , as people age , there is an increased need for more healthcare resources , hence leading to inevitable spending in healthcare .
However , for the elderly with low or no income , they may face difficulties and restrictions in seeking appropriate medical treatment due to financial problems .
It is therefore crucial for governments to extend assistance to the elderly in the form of medical subsidies , allowances , rebates and other kinds of financial aid to ensure that the needs of the elderly are met and protected .
In contrast to the above , although public spending on the aged is essential , there is also a need for investment of resources in the educational sector to provide better education for the younger generation .
As the common saying goes , " Children are the future pillar of the nation " .
They should be given every right to receive education under any circumstances .
Resources of the country should be diverted into this aspect to provide scholarships and bursaries for needy children who are keen to further their studies and allow them to be exposed to better working opportunities in the future .
Not only that , an educated population will also mean a more productive workforce for the country , which will allow them to have an edge over others .
Moreover , as mentioned by Friedland and Summer ( 2005 ) , " Public policies that encourage and facilitate education , basic research , and the application of promising technologies can enhance the well-being of current and future generations of older people " .
This further suggests that with education , older people in the future will be more affluent and will have a lesser tendency to rely on the government for their well-being .
In turn , the government will not need to set aside huge resources for the aged , and will be able to divert these resources into other areas of the country 's development .
In addition to education , infrastructure of a country also plays a crucial role in drawing in investments and potential profits for the country .
With a pro-business infrastructure , not only will Meetings , Incentives , Conventions and Exhibitions ( MICE ) groups will be enticed to make more frequent visits to the country , there will also be a rapid increase of Small and Medium Enterprises ( SME ) within the country as local entrepreneurs will be more willing to venture and establish businesses in a pro-business climate .
Moreover , foreign investors may also seek the opportunity to invest in the country which generally contributes to the overall economic growth of the country as a whole .
In short , a better suggestion will be for countries to place more emphasis on its economical development , as with economic growth , people are generally more affluent and hence will possess the financial capabilities of providing for themselves in old age .
Even though there will also be a minority who are less capable of doing that , they will only comprise of the few who require assistance from the government .
Just a few decades ago , most economies were so focused in developing their country economically that they ignored the issue of an aging population .
However , this issue of a graying population has become a world phenomenon now .
Yet , it was only until recently that governments , especially those in developed countries , started to pay much more attention to the problem .
In today 's globalized world where global economies are interdependent on each other , the undesirable ripple effect brought about by an aging population is not to be under estimated .
For this will eventually beset and hinder not only a country 's development but also slowdown the pace of the world 's economic growth as well .
Undoubtedly , more public spending should be channeled to deal with the issues piggybacked along with a graying population .
As such public spending on other areas of the country 's development such as education would need to be cut to maintain a budget balance .
Nevertheless , public spending on the aged should be limited so that money can be diverted to other areas of the country 's development .
In today 's globalized world , where economies are growing at a tremendous rate in a diverse way , national spending has to be planned concisely and utilized fully in order to achieve optimal economic growth .
With an aging population , the government has to direct more resources to take care of the needs of the elderly in areas such as medical care .
With public spending reduced in areas such as education and housing so as to fund the elderly , the country 's development would be hindered by the lack of funds .
Eventually , this will lead to a less thriving economy , which can pose a major problem to a country .
As supported by Peterson ( 2001 ) in his article , 'A Graying World ' , the government 'must realize that any further expansion of retirement benefits would only impoverish future generations and limit future public spending .
' Resources available to a country are scarce and limited and the government should not compromise the budget spent on other areas to fund the aged instead .
With an aging workforce , productivity of the workers will be reduced .
This would greatly affect a country 's development as there are not enough people to fuel the economy .
This is especially true in countries such as Singapore , where its economic growth is largely dependent on its people .
The issue of an aging population will pose a threat to her future economic development .
Therefore , the government has come up with policies such as increasing the retirement age for the working age population ; re-skilling so as to help them keep up with the new technology required in their field of work .
The underlying objective of implementing such policies is to prolong the period of time that the aging workforce can assist the country for progression .
For this reason , public spending on the aged can not and should not be limited .
Nonetheless , the government should fund the age with a perimeter in mind for such heavy subsidies comes with heavy tax levies citizens and investors .
With high personal and corporate tax , citizens may end up migrating to other countries causing a brain drain .
Investors may well eventually be deterred from investing in the country .
Moreover , the aged may become over dependent on the government for subsidies .
In the long run , these are unhealthy and detrimental to a country 's development .
Therefore , polices to encourage self-reliance can be implemented to shift the burden of financial aid required when one ages to the individual .
One good example is the implementation of Medisave in Singapore .
Instead , funds should be diverted to other areas such as building more industrial infrastructures so as to attract more foreign-direct investments for the country 's development .
Hence , public spending on the aged should be limited .
Ultimately , the issue of a graying population can not be solved immediately .
While it seems that it is necessary for a government to increase public spending to fund the aged so that productivity of the workers can be maintained , this can not be sustained in the long run .
Governments should not be myopic and divert most of the funds to support the aged but instead weigh the pros and cons carefully when implementing various long terms polices .
References : Peter G. Peterson , A Graying World .
Peter G. Peterson.Harvard International Review .
23 .
3 ( Fall 2001 ) : 66 .
In today 's context , ever changing societies frequently face a plethora of issues .
Likewise , most countries are currently tackling the main issue of an increasing ageing population .
Since today 's silver population of 65 years and above is 15 percent and is estimated to grow phenomenally to around 25 percent by the year 2030 ( Centre for Strategic and International Studies 2009 ) , governments are deeply concerned with the implementation of fiscal and welfare policies that would directly impact the aged .
A component of fiscal policy , public spending , should be limited so that self-reliance can be inculcated into the aged through forced savings and so the economy can function better by shifting monetary input to facilitate trade and investments in infrastructure .
Contrary to limiting public spending , critics of human rights have been advocating that state funding should not be minimized at the expense of growing the economy .
As it is often said that the most valuable resources of a country are its people , so , the particular group of people who had initially shaped the formative years of a country are now well into their aging years where they are valued and appreciated as a result of their hard work and contribution .
The mindset of respecting those who built the country from scratch resonates with feeling indebted to take care of them when they are old and providing them with the best of health-care .
Thus , in this instance , financing the elderly is an obligation in which public spending should not be cut back just because the aging population is growing at an alarming rate .
Moreover , to a large extent , the government should favour funding programmes and policies for the elderly so their interests are well looked after .
Although it has always been the case that an appropriate amount of public spending is set aside for the aged , it is of the utmost importance to instill a sense of self-reliance in them , which can be effective through education about saving for retirement throughout their working life .
This plan of accumulating savings by an individual is compelled by law in some countries and at the same time , while the savings can help to enhance one 's responsibility for their own life , they can also provide people with financial security after retirement .
In this way , the aged would not be too dependent on family and the state for financial assistance .
Furthermore , when public spending is minimized through self-reliance on the part of individuals to save for their future , the money can instead be put to better use to help those who really need it .
Another point in case is that since the elderly can be self-sufficient to support themselves in old age , public spending in this area is thus limited , and may eventually result in a shift of state funds and capital to boost other sectors of the country 's economy .
As such , there may be a huge concentration of growth in the area of trade because when more capital is used to invest in trade , it reaps substantial rewards in the form of revenue and higher growth and stability of the economy will ensue .
Besides state funds and capital can be used to improve the infrastructure of the country ; e.g .
public transport systems , telecommunications sector , energy and water resource divisions etc , so that the country may advance and prosper with the advent of new technologies and infrastructure .
As a whole , the notion of public spending can be viewed as akin to a coin which has two sides .
One aspect is that of favouring the elderly to the extent of fulfilling their needs to the best the state can provide because of their esteemed position as 'forefathers ' of the country .
Whereas , the other aspect is seen as benefitting the whole community at large since such capital and funds are diverted to improve the economy .
Henceforth , the problems associated with an aging society is one such situation which can not be dismissed lightly as having a definite solution , as often there are severe repercussions that occur with it .
