Nowadays , balancing public spending is a big concern to most of governments since they need to carefully evaluate the most suitable spending areas for short and long-term plans .
Failure to make decisions in government budget will affect whole countries ' systems including their economy... , education , or environment , etc .
This essay argues that public spending on the aged , one of the most major categories that have recently invested in , should be limited for developing other countries ' important areas , promoting their sustainable growths , and forming people 's responsibilities .
First of all , large spending on the aged results in an imbalance of governments ' expenditure .
According to World Midyear Population by Age and Sex of US Census Bureau , the population aged above 60 will rise from 20.38 % in 2009 to 21.97 % in 2020 , which means more governments ' are spending on pensions and healthcare .
When the aged public spending has increased to a considerable proportion , governments will find it difficult to keep pace with the advances in technologies , commerce , and education , etc. For instance , a recent Singapore government list in 2008 shows that pensions have been accounted for $ 912 billion that has been equal to 17.8 % in total expense .
And the largest category is health care with $ 925 billion , which was nearly spent on an elder assistance such as home nursing , medical treatment , and long-term care , etc .
In contrast , education , welfare , and environment , which are essential for countries ' development , have only ranked as 3rd , 4th , and 8th categories respectively .
In the same way , Australia has spent nearly 10 % on healthcare while USA has spent about 15 % .
As suggested , " if the government does nothing it will experience a rise in the structural budget deficit .
This has many problems no full-stop
" ( Pettinger , 2007 ) .
One of the challenges is the increase in the national debt and annual interest payments .
For example , Italy has already borrowed over 100 % of GDP , resulting in a high % of GDP goes on servicing the debt , which will only increase over time .
Secondly , what I want to emphasize is that the governments should only limit the public expense , which does not mean that they totally abandon the aged .
There are still activities carried out to help the elderly such as providing free travel on public transportation in cities or subsidizing old people 's homes , etc .
Opponents of this position argue that the elderly should be paid attention with more and more subsidies because they are the predecessors with a lot of commitment to societies .
However , limiting expenditure on the elder enables countries to invest more in another aspects like economy , education , environment or infrastructure .
Then the countries will be stronger and stronger , supporting the aged with large amount of money .
We agree with Chan ( 2009 ) , who concluded : " ... " .
Thirdly , less spending on the aged helps decrease the proportion of dependence rate .
In 2009 , 11 % is the percentage of Asia older dependency , which is anticipated to reach 28 % in 2040 .
Similarly , the older dependency ratio of Western Europe is projected to increase by approximately 50 % from 27 % to 53 % .
It goes on to reinstate the elder do count more on the state ; subsequently , more resources must be diverted into caring for them .
Hence , the government should limit the aged subsidies in order to stimulate their responsibilities .
In addition , requiring people to save money in advance can be the incentives for their independence .
" They should be encouraged or compelled by law to save and invest more of their income earned while they are active in workforce " .
( Peterson , 2009 ) .
Peterson also adds that governments and policy makers need to take in to careful consideration what is essentially supported by the public sector and what should depend on their own financial capacities .
All in all , every country has to formulate and implement the long-term public spending strategy , which mainly depends on different countries ' economic , social and education backgrounds .
And restricting the cost on the aged in order to advance other necessary areas is a difficult decision for them .
Nevertheless , it is a critical and ultimate path to their full development .
