In Singapore today , our median age is 36 years and life expectancy is 80 years .
The median age has significantly increased from 24 years since 1980 , and predicted to reach 54 years within the next 40 years , similar to that of Japan and Italy now .
( Singapore Department of Statistics , 1999 , Lee , 2009 ) There will be increased burden on the working adult population to provide for the aged dependents , be it healthcare or finances .
This is made worse with the shrinking workforce that we are now experiencing .
However , current research and developments , and polices have effectively help eased the aging process .
This is done through technological advances in medical care , policies to encourage financial independency among the elderly , and adopting a foreign talent policy .
Firstly , recent research and developments have resulted in higher levels of medical care .
For example , new and better treatment methods like open heart surgery or radiotherapy help increase the chances of recovery and decrease time required for recovery from illnesses .
New vaccines and immunizations site are constantly developed to help prevent diseases at an early stage too .
In 2008 , a team of researchers from Wayne State University successfully developed a breast cancer vaccine that is able to treat women with treatment-resistant HER2-positive breast cancer , and prevent cancer from occurring again .
( Evans , 2008 ) This creates two benefits pertaining to aging population .
One , the population will be generally healthier , with a smaller tendency to fall sick , which otherwise disrupts productivity of the economy and increases the need for medical services and facilities .
Next , by reducing the recovery period for illnesses , people who are ill , especially the elderly population who are more prone to sickness , will require less treatment sessions or usage of hospital facilities and thus incur less medical expenses too .
One may argue that the latest medical treatment methods tend to be very costly and the average elderly in Singapore may not be able to afford them .
This problem is solved with the use of subsides for elderly by the government , to keep medical fees for these new treatment methods within an affordable range .
This would also be just considered as a short term measure , taking into account future medical advances .
More cost efficient methods for the same treatment would be developed which lower treatment costs to a point where only minimal subsides is required .
Secondly , policies to encourage financial independency among the elderly , for instance CPF ( Central Provident Fund ) , Medishield and the extended retirement age from 60 to 62 years in 1999 .
( Ministry of Manpower , 2006 ) By increasing the retirement age , it creates a motivating effect on people to work till at later age , so as to enjoy benefits like pensions and government incentives after retirement .
They would also be able to obtain more wealth which they could use to support themselves in old age .
This helps lessen the government 's burden to provide for the elderly , and the effects of a shrinking workforce brought by aging population .
Companies would also be able to benefit more from the experience and expertise of their more elder employees .
In addition , elderly workers tend to have lower turnover rates for employment , which is beneficial to the company .
For example Wal-mart , the world 's largest public corporation by revenue , actively hires elderly workers for her operations -- close to 20 % of Wal- mart 's workforce is at least 55 years old .
( Walter , 2006 ) One may argue that this in a sense forces later retirement , especially for people with insufficient savings to depend on without a job .
However it also emphasizes a greater need to be financially independent and helps create more elderly involvement in the society which contributes to the economy .
Thirdly , foreign talent policy , of which the government embraces , also plays an important role in easing the aging process .
Foreign talents help increase the workforce in the economy , which helps to solve the problem of shrinking workforce brought about by aging population .
Effective use of their expertise also helps create a more productive economy and generate more economic income which could be diverted to helping the elderly population .
More importantly , they create greater diversity and especially vibrancy in the country , of which lack of it is evidently seen in some countries in Europe due to a decreasing proportion of youths brought about by aging population .
A recent study also indicated a 170 percentage increase in Singapore 's non-resident workforce over the last decade , from 248,000 in 1990 to 670,000 in 2006 .
( Yeoh , 2007 ) One of the most common arguments against the policy would be that the increased competitiveness of employment which results in loss of jobs for the local population , even with the implementation of foreign employment quota ratios by the government .
However , from a different viewpoint , the increased competition results in greater competitiveness of our firms not only within Singapore 's economy but globally as well .
This policy would also be vital for Singapore to sustain strong economic growth and competitiveness in the global market in the future .
All in all , current research and development , and policies have been successful in easing the aging process , through advancements in healthcare , encouraging financial interdependency and embracing foreign talent .
In the years to come , we will face greater challenges as we gradually experience the full impact of the aging population , of which new strategies will have to be integrated along with the current ones to meet the changing circumstances that we might face .
