In 2009 , the median age of Singaporeans was 36 years .
By 2050 , the same figure is expected to increase to 54 years ( Lee , 2009 ) , as a result of lower birth fertility and higher life expectancies .
The predicament of an aging population plagues not only Singapore , but also different countries , ranging from the richer and developed nations , such as Japan and Italy , to the relatively poorer and developing nations like China ( , 2009 ) , especially as a result of the one-child policy .
While the issues of an aging population are relevant and dynamic , it must be noted that countries at different stages of development will place emphasis on different developmental goals and adapt policies that caters to the needs of the nation .
This paper , henceforth , aims to discuss the impact of resources allocation in the context of developed nations , countries with stable economic basis and decent standards in the various aspects pertaining to a nation 's progress , such as infrastructures and amenities .
Therefore , there is no heavy emphasis on developing the economic , educational and political systems .
Some may point to the fact that providing for the aged drains a nation of its resources and reduces resources for future generations , but the whole idea is fundamentally flawed .
As a fellow human being , compassion for others necessitates to care for them .
As the younger generation , ethics bound to provide for them .
As a country progressing towards a gracious society and thus development , rationality leads towards addressing these aging issues .
With that said , it is important to address the need of an aging population and not be overly concerned with the fiscal costs of an aging population .
( Friedland and Summer , 2005 )
Critics of this position argue that the burden of the fiscal and economic costs associated with an aging population is so large and substantial that it is unsustainable , and therefore , should be limited .
( Peterson , 2001 ) Indeed , the funding allocated to the aging population will place a heavier burden on the shrinking workforce .
Tax increase is likely to significantly increase over the next two decades , affecting a firm 's labour costs and indirectly , consumers ' income .
( Clark , Ogawa & Mason , 2007 ) Although the fall in productivity labour force is likely to influence economic growth as well , there are other means a firm can employ to stay competitive and sustain or even increase productivity .
These may include researching and developing better methodologies or exploiting greater economic of scales , eventually sustaining the fiscal policies associated with an aging population .
Countries are also raising the retirement age .
For example , Japan expects to raise the retirement age to 65 by 2013 .
( Gross & Minot , 2008 ) This implies that older people are still able to contribute to the workforce .
It also indicates that medical advances has helped people stay healthy , meaning to say that healthcare costs for the aged may not end up being astronomical .
On the contrary , if issues related to the aging population are not dealt with appropriately , the social impact on a nation 's development may be hindered .
Every individual should have compassion for a fellow human being to care and provide for the aged .
The amount of public spending should not be an object .
The elderly population is still part of mankind , part of the society and part of the nation .
It is a gracious society to care for who are unable to provide for themselves .
Sights of a forsaken aged population are heart wrenching and affects the manner external parties , for example , trading partners , view the nation .
Furthermore , an established welfare system will reflect the societal values that are attributes of a first class nation .
Hence , in order to ensure that the aged are adequately provided for , that the nation will be able to develop the nation as a gracious society , public spending in these areas must be sufficient .
Furthermore , the younger generation has to fulfil a moral obligation towards the older generation , and capping public spending limits their abilities to do so .
It is important to sufficiently provide for the older generation , for they were the ones who toiled and contributed to shape the progress of the nation today .
Therefore , it is only ethical that their efforts are reciprocated .
One exemplary example is the samsui women .
They were the ones who had toiled in the sun to build the infrastructure during Singapore 's early developing days .
They are older single women who are uneducated and were not able to save , that the government should especially provide help for .
( Friedland and Summer , 2005 ) It would be simply inhumane to cast them aside and overlook the fact that they have been vital in the developmental process .
By setting a limit on public spending , many other areas of developments in a nation are not explored .
The aged are a great asset to the nation , in terms of their expertise and experience .
In addition to their past contributions , the aged are likely to be able to continue contributing to the society , by simply passing on their wealth of experience or adding diversity and vibrancy to a nation .
They can also serve as the pillar of a family , binding family units and improving social cohesiveness .
In view of their past and potential contributions in developing the nations , limiting public spending on the aged may actually impede the progress of the nation .
Moreover , inadequate policies will lead to political and social instabilities for moral and ethical issues so , by not setting a limit on public spending on the aged , it indirectly helps to sustain and even improve the social cohesiveness and development in this aspect .
Limiting public spending will instead create unintended costs , such as unhappiness amongst people .
Otherwise , like Friedland & Summer ( 2005 ) mentioned , cutting back on public spending on welfare programmes has an impact on local families and communities .
Based on the above discussed , the aged should be well taken care of .
It is understood that there will be a cost in providing for the aged .
However , to limit the public spending on policies to deal with an aging population effectively translates to inadequacy in catering to an aging population .
The world 's population is continually growing , not just in term of size but also in the sense that it is growing older .
( , 2008 ) .
Therefore , there is a rising importance to deal with the issues related to aging population .
In evaluating the policies to undertake , it is necessary to consider the net impact of using public funds to deal with the aging population , as well as the consequences if the issues relating to aging population are not adequately dealt with .
Public spending on the aged should therefore not be limited as investing time and resources to solving aging population eliminates any unnecessary social costs and contributes to the development of the society as a whole , promoting cohesiveness and progress towards a gracious society .
