As one of the most rapid aging population in Asia , Singapore encounters the challenge of developing policies to accommodate this evolving age structure .
Now , people who are living longer and longer lives must carefully plan their lives. .
It is a necessity to raise awareness concerning the issue of aging population .
Welcoming immigrants and increasing fertility rates are some of the issues that should become national priority and these subjects will be discussed in relation to the Singapore context .
Although the relieving of the aging process is unlikely to take effect immediately after the implementation of the policies , much can still be done to ease the impact of an aging population .
Firstly , the aging process should be eased by allowing more working-age immigrants .
Minister Mentor , Lee Kuan Yew ( ) mentioned that there are complains about the welcoming of working-age immigrants resulting in a reduction of job vacancies available for Singaporeans .
Immigrants who have the skills and expertise in the various industries are favoured by employers .
This is because they can contribute their knowledge and bring the companies to greater heights .
Hence , this has caused Singaporeans who are not as proficient to become unemployed .
The introduction of working-age immigrants has also increased the competition in the workforce .
Eventually , social issues like a surge in suicidal cases and psychological problems will surface .
Furthermore , immigrants who set up businesses or companies in Singapore would create more job vacancies .
They could share their knowledge and technology with Singapore and Singapore could in return , provide them the necessary infrastructure and manpower .
This will certainly be a win-win situation where both Singaporeans and immigrants will benefit .
In addition , competition in the labor force might stimulate people to be more eager and determined to improve themselves .
They will be shocked by the competitiveness and drive themselves to perform better .
This will bring about a progress in the overall standard of the country and the economy .
Secondly , the process of aging should be relieved by increasing fertility rates .
With the influx of immigrants and foreign talents , most Singaporeans believe that Singapore 's current population is considerably large .
Minister Mentor , Lee Kuan Yew ( ) pointed out that between the ages of 25 and 40 , more than 30 % of the Singaporeans remain single and childless .
Many Singaporeans do not want their careers to be hindered and deem it is not worth to pay the high fees of educating their children .
Despite the introduction of the baby bonus scheme , Singaporeans are still unmoved and cold toward having a child .
However , increasing the fertility rates to replace the aging population in Singapore is vital .
Being a small country , Singapore is vulnerable to many threats and people is her only resource .
If the low fertility rate continues , Singapore 's economy and labour force will eventually shrink .
Thus , it is essential to introduce campaigns and educate Singaporeans on the importance of family formation .
The government could look into ways like longer maternity leave , financial supports in the form of tax rebates and maintaining work-life balance by allocating sufficient time for personal life .
Numerous countries experience aging population
The older population is expanding at a substantially faster rate than that of the world 's total population .
Presently , the total fertility rate is below the replacement level in essentially all industrialized countries .
The dramatic shift in age structure has caused the Singapore government to introduce many related policies .
Singapore 's response has been to welcome immigrants , introduce baby bonus and many more .
In conclusion , the easing of the aging process should be a national effort .
The future is to assure that people everywhere age with confidence and courtliness and continue to enjoy full rights .
In the last 10 years , governments all over the world , especially those in developed countries such as Japan , United Kingdom , Singapore , etc , invested heavily in research and development and formulating new policies to tackle the prevailing ageing phenomenon which is threatening their economies .
However , some policies introduced by the government are causing much problems for the society which outweighs the effect of easing the ageing process .
In this paper , two strategies , one of which encourages older retirement and the other leads to the increase in foreign immigration , introduced by the local government to tackle the ageing population issue shall be discussed .
In 2007 , the government introduced a CPF ( Central Provident Fund ) reform scheme , which will push retirement age to 65 years in 2012 which will then be on par with Japan .
This , coupled with other initiatives such as the workfare to older workers and incentives for companies to hire elderly workers , serves to encourage longer working lives and decrease dependency among elderly workers .
However , according to the Singapore National Census in 2007 , 68 % of the elder workers are unskilled and most of them are employed as cleaners or laborers , working long hours per day for mostly as little as $ 5 an hour ( 2009 ) .
However , many might argue that through employment , older workers will be able to earn their upkeep , save up for future medical needs and live an active and healthy lifestyle ( 2009 ) .
But on the contrary , it has been statistically proven that older workers tend to suffer from occupational injuries that were inflicted and accumulated since younger working days , thus accidents are more prone to occur , especially for jobs which require substantial amount of physical strength such as lifting or carrying ( 1996 ) .
Besides , elderly workers can get abused just as much as younger workers , by considering the fact that if elder workers were to be dismissed for some reasons , finding another employment with equal remunerations and similar job tasks might not be easy .
In addition , if they were to be subjected to demands of work performance and long work hours in most jobs , how could they ever live a healthier lifestyle ?
On the contrary , older workers could develop more medical problems as they continue to work and grow older .
Therefore , it can be said that governments who support older retirement age could have committed the fallacy of equating both wealth and health together when it is the other way around .
Singapore has been adopting an " open-door " policy which favors foreign immigration as part of the strategy to overcome ageing population .
With a healthy supply of young workers from other countries , Singapore can fill up job vacancies quickly and replace those older workers .
As the saying , to kill two birds with one stone , it can be said that foreign immigration solves both the ageing problem as well as the shortage of babies. .
However , with the influx of foreigners into the economy , cultural clashes will occur and more often than not , misunderstandings due to language differences arise .
According to a survey published by the Institute of Service Excellence ( ISES ) last year , Singapore has a lower service level satisfactory score of 68 compared to 72 in South Korea and 75 in the States . One of the reasons is the ease of foreign immigration here .
Nowadays , many semi-skilled foreign immigrants are working in service sectors , and even greet many customers in their mother tongue .
As a result , tourists and even mainland Singaporeans are often put off by their incomprehensible enunciation and insolent intonation of their native language .
Thus , businesses in these sectors employing lower cost foreign workers might lose the goodwill of customers .
Therefore , in the long term , it could hurt the service industry 's economies and so , this policy contradicts the main objective of having a strong labor economy .
Singapore 's policy to tackle the ageing issue has so far achieved limited success but the side-effects caused by their implementations have greater adversities which outweigh the benefits .
There can never be a single solution or working policy in the battle against ageing population but however , besides the government , each and every individual can actually play a part for their future .
After all , we all grow old one day .
