The usage of the government budget has been a concern among politicians .
Decisions on the allocation of government funds can impact different aspects of society differently .
So how would spending money on the elderly affect society ?
Should the government continue to finance elderly welfare ?
Some believe that government spending on the elderly should be controlled and that the money should be used in other aspects of the society such as education , economic growth and public health care .
Governments should limit their spending on the elderly as public spending may cause problems .
Continuous funding on elderly welfare may cause the elderly to be over reliant on welfare .
Thus , if governments were to reduce elderly welfare in the future due to circumstances like serious budget deficit , these over dependent elderly would face difficulties in their life .
Sociologists argue that government spending on the aged should not be limited as there is a portion of the elderly , such as the frail elderly without family support , that really needs help ( Robert B. Friedman and Laura Summer , 2005 ) .
However , the community and not only the government can help the weak elderly in society
The Community Chest is one channel whereby the public can donate money to finance welfare programs for the elderly .
This shows that governments can afford to reduce spending on the elderly as welfare is partially funded by the community .
Therefore , governments should limit their spending on the aged .
Also , Governments should use the government budget to deal with other issues that are more pressing than the aging population .
Currently , the proportion of the elderly in the society is still relatively small in most countries like Japan and Singapore so the problem is not that serious yet .
Thus , the government should use the money to handle more pressing issue such as the health of the general public in this period whereby there are outbreaks of a lot of diseases such as avian flu and swine flu , that have taken the lives of many .
Some politicians may argue that although the proportion of elderly people is still small now , it will grow larger if the problem of aging population is not dealt with now .
However , there are other ways to deal with the problems of aging population rather than financing the welfare of elderly which will reduce the amount available to spend on more pressing issues now .
One of the ways would be to stress filial obligation and increase the willingness of children to support their elderly parents ( Peter G. Peterson , 2001 ) .
If this does not work , the government could legalize filial obligation .
The Maintenance of Parents act in Singapore requires children to support their elderly parents .
Since there are definitely other ways to cope with aging populations governments should allocate its funds to other areas that are more urgent .
Furthermore , Governments should focus on other areas such as education and economic growth , as tackling such areas will solve the problem of an aging population in the long run , killing two birds with one stone .
Education will increase productivity of people and this will lead to economic growth that will ensure the continuation of welfare programs for the elderly ( Robert B. Friedman and Laura Summer , 2005 ) .
Increase in economic growth will also lead to an increase in employment .
Thus more people are able to work and accumulate savings for their old age .
Critics argue that people tend to spend instead of saving .
This may be true but there are policies such as the Central Provident Fund ( CPF ) scheme that compel people , by law , to save while they are active in the workforce ( Peter G. Peterson , 2001 ) .
In this scheme , a portion of a person 's monthly salary will be deposited into the CPF account on a monthly basis and it can only be withdrawn at the age of 55 , thereby assuring financial independence of the elderly .
Therefore , governments should reduce their spending on the elderly and more of the budget should be used on education and for boosting economic growth .
In conclusion , public funding should be limited as it may cause the elderly to be over reliant on it . The money should then be used elsewhere in areas such as public health that are more pressing as well as on education and economic growth as that will also help to solve the aging population crisis .
By carefully allocating government funds , society will benefit as a whole .
In developed countries , problems of aging populations are surfacing and at the same time worsening .
Limitations on public spending on the aged should be warded off .
The main focus of this essay will be on developed countries .
The reason for this is because their aging population problem is much more serious as compared to developing countries .
There is a need to understand that the amount of reliance the aged have on the public is quite significant . It is also necessary to take into account the contributions that the aged have made , as well as to recognize the more substantial advancement in other main areas of development over the aged .
Critics have commented that other areas of the country 's development such as education need the money to substantiate the advancement of these particular developments so as to bring them to greater heights .Therefore , limitations on public spending on the aged should be enforced .
However , there is the recognition of the fact that governments in developed countries have already pumped in a huge amount of money to develop the main areas of development like education and national defence .
Thus , these main areas have been well-developed .
In addition , an aging population has not been an area of concern in the past and only recently have the aged formed a significant population in developed countries .
Thus more should be spent on the aged since other areas are already so well developed that there is not really a need to use money to substantiate their advancement .
It is well known that Singapore has a world class education system .
From the past till now , Singapore has been spending a huge sum of money on the other main areas of development so as to build up the economy .
The efforts can be seen clearly as Singapore is now an advanced developing country . However the most predominant problem arising now is the aging population .
Thus public spending on the aged must not be limited as it is a arising prevailing problem .
Cynics have mentioned that in developed countries , quite a number of the aged are still working and receiving wages , so they will have the means to pay for their living expenses and healthcare costs .
Thus public spending on the aged can be limited so that other areas like national defence can have the money to further their advancement .
However even though a significant number of the aged are still in the workforce , most of them are taking up menial jobs like cleaning which only gives them a meagre pay .
This small amount of money is hardly enough to cover their living expenses and healthcare costs as now healthcare costs are increasingly higher .
In addition , the aged are still quite vulnerable to diseases and are weak , thus public spending on the aged should not be limited so as to assist them with their healthcare costs .
This is so as the aged are not suitable for laborious jobs .
The fact that the aged still need to work despite their vulnerability implies that they might be forced to work to meet their own basic needs .
This further infers that there is not enough public spending on the aged . This is another reason why public spending should not be limited on the aged .
Developed countries are implementing measures to meet the needs of the aged .
For instance in Singapore , Prime Minister Lee Hsien Loong mentioned in the recent National Day Rally Speech ( Goh , 2009 , para 19 ) that with the population rapidly graying , the Singapore government will put healthcare costs at the front and centre of its agenda .
This is a precise indication that the Singapore 's government does not have the intention to limit public spending on the aged .
The main reason for this phenomenon is that money needs to be spent on the aged now so as to ease the aging process , if not , years down the road , public spending might have to increase significantly as compared to now due to the lack of spending on the aged .
Thus it is important to spend more now to ease the problems of aging population , if not years later , the problems of an aging population might heap up .
Engelhard ( 2009 ) mentioned that the aged are not worth the attention from the public .
They are deemed useless and with low productivity they can not contribute significantly to society
Thus society should limit their spending on the aged and shift their focus onto the young .
In time to come , the young can give back to society unlike the aged .
However the fact that the aged were young once and also made contributions to the society also needs to be recognized .
In fact they are the ones who laid the foundations for the younger generations .
Without their contributions , the country may not be where and what it is now , thus it is society 's responsibility to take care of them , regarding this as a repayment back to the aged .
This is like a cycle : in the past when the aged are still working they were the ones who took care of the younger generations , thus now it is the younger generation 's responsibility to take care of the aged .
In Japan it is predicted that the median age is expected to increase by a decade , from just over 43 years in 2007 to more than 52 years in 2050 .
( Smil , 2007 , para 9 ) This shows that public spending on the aged should not be limited as the percentage of the aged will increase significantly in time to come due to the problems arising from an aging population .
By 2055 many more people will require elderly friendly facilities .
Limitations should not be encouraged as years later the demand for elderly friendly facilities might exceed the supply of them .
Therefore it is important to spend on the aged now to ease the aging process years later .
In a nutshell , the above mentioned arguments and reasons point to the fact that public spending on the aged should not be limited as if it is limited , dire consequences ,such as the country itself might be seen as neglecting the aged , will surface .
This might lead to foreign corporations being discouraged to invest in Singapore as Singapore might be perceived as not having an effective system . Therefore it can be concluded that the aged should have a bigger portion of the pie that is the public spending , while other areas will still have their share to maintain their standards in developed countries .
