Rogers , E. M. ( 2003 ) .
The generation of innovations .
In Diffusion of innovations ( pp.136-157 ) .
New York : Free Press .
Hopes from Toshiba Laptops
The development of technology has benefited human beings in both scientific research and daily life of human beings .
With the invention of the microscope , scientists are able to observe molecules which form all matter .
Besides scientific research , inventions and new technologies have indeed brought convenience to human beings .
With mobile phones and Internet around , individuals are connected to the whole world with a simple click .
As Rogers stated in his book , development of technologies is stimulated either by scientific research or by social reasons .
( Rogers , 2003 ) One important technology which was socially determined is the invention of the laptop in Toshiba , a Japanese electronics company .
The invention of the laptop was originally designed to fit crowded offices and small homes which were typical in Japan .
At that time , dozens of workers shared one desk and desktop computers were too large in size .
( Rogers , 2003 ) The laptop was then invented by an engineer in Toshiba Company called Mizoguchi , whose braveness and wisdom have been recognized in the process of inventing the laptop .
However , because of lacking resources and unpromising market , the innovation of the laptop in Toshiba was nearly aborted .
Fortunately , the designers of the laptop were determined so people nowadays are able to access the Internet anywhere with laptops that were first invented by them .
In the process of inventing a laptop , the design team was lacking in human resource and money .
At first , the idea of designing laptop was disapproved by the Toshiba Company , which at that time had just suffered from a huge loss in the business of personal computers .
( Rogers , 2003 ) Corporate leaders of the company then were no longer interested in computer-related businesses and thus refused to provide fund and experts to the designing team .
Although the team survived due to the insistence of Mizogushi and finally came up with the first laptop in the world , lack of resources had had an impact on the design team , which made the team 'stressful ' ( Rogers , 2003 ) in the designing process .
After the laptop was invented , it was forbidden to be sold in Japan since the company was not optimistic about the market of its new product .
( Rogers , 2003 ) Thus , the designers decided to present their production in Europe first , where they had a great success .
After that , laptops were introduced to all parts of the world and had brought convenience to people .
To wrap up , the invention of the laptop was affected by the lack of resources and unpromising market which ultimately came from opinion of the authority in Toshiba Company which turned out to be incorrect .
However , the result is that laptops were eventually invented and brought convenience to human beings .
It is true that sometimes technology innovation may be affected by some factors , but hopes are that determined engineers such as Mizogushi will eventually come up with inventions that can improve our quality of life .
References :
Rogers , E.M. ( 2003 ) .
The Birth of the Laptop Computer at Toshiba .
In Diffusion of innovations ( pp.144-146 ) .
New York : Free Press .
The Serendipitous Discovery of Penicillin
Serendipitous scientific discoveries often refer to those unexpected surprises scientists came across fully by chance in the area of science and technology .
These unplanned serendipitous " accidents " often turned out to be good results .
Actually many major discoveries made by excellent scientists are unexpected gifts from good luck .
Some of them did change the course of history and still have a significant influence on the entire human race today , even the whole environment where we settled in .
For instance , the discovery of cosmic background radiation , the invention of the first microwave oven and the discovery of electromagnetism are all serendipitous .
In particular , pharmacology is perhaps the most common field where serendipity occurs .
The discovery of penicillin--a fairly common antibiotic in drug stores--is one of the most famous .
Alexander Fleming was a British bacteriologist ( also a pharmacologist ) who served as a captain in an army medical corps during World War I .
He had been searching for antibiotic substances that can attack a variety of bacteria since his graduation .
When he served in the army , he noticed that many soldiers died from simple infections caused by small wounds .
In fact , at that time doctors had no cure for infections .
Every day hospitals were filled with patients who only got infections from a small cut or a scratch and more seriously those who contracted pneumonia or tuberculosis .
However , doctors and nurses could do little to save their life .
Fleming spared no effort to carry on his experiments and research in this area , hoping that he could find a cure for such simple but lethal infections .
In September 1928 , having returned from his vacation , Fleming continued with the work in his lab immediately .
Before leaving for the holiday , he stacked a pile of staphylococcus cultures in the lab .
After he came back , he began to sort through the dishes containing those cultures in the sink .
To his surprise he suddenly found that there was a dot on one culture around which the colonies of the bacteria were damaged , while those colonies further away were normal .
It was a mould growing there , which was supposed to happen because Fleming 's lab was often untidy .
However , the major difference was that the bacteria he had been growing on those dishes were staphylococci , which can cause infections in human body .
With a sharp scientifically trained mind , Fleming realized that there must be something inside the fungus or released by it killing the bacteria around it .
He examined the mould and identified that it belongs to the penicillium family .
The mould released some kind of " mould juice " that can stop the bacteria 's growth .
After several months he named the substance Penicillin .
Following some subsequent investigations , he published his findings in a medical journal , with prediction of this new substance 's potential use in medication .
After the discovery of the new substance , he came to the problem of how to cultivate the mould and how to isolate and purify the antibiotic agent .
Both of them seemed to be quite difficult .
Fleming continued his research and trial for a long period of time with his assistant , but to no avail .
During World War II , the work was taken over by two other scientists and they soon found a technique to purify penicillin and started the mass production .
It saved many soldiers ' lives in the war .
Of course Fleming 's contribution was never forgotten .
He shared the Nobel Prize for physiology/medicine with the two scientists .
It is quite interesting that penicillin was once discovered by a scientist serendipitously .
What would have happened if Fleming had never been concerned about the search for the cure or he had had no interest in this area at all ?
What would have happened if Fleming had ignored the contamination and just dropped it into the cleaning mixture ?
The whole world would have waited for more decades to see the discovery of penicillin .
Hard work finally paid off .
Serendipity only favours those with high aspirations and determination who whole-heartedly dedicate themselves to their beloved career .
What hampers engineering design processes for innovation in China ?
Century after century the engineering design processes for innovations speed up all-round social progress and create the civilisation we have today .
As far as I am concerned , the so-called engineering design processes for innovations actually means that the whole industry depends on the creative and passionate engineers to design new products or systems which can perfectly meet the society 's demand .
In this way , the society is pushed forward .
That is why we say that scientific and technological process and innovation is the basic motive to promote productivity development .
However nowadays , we may find that these processes seem not so significant .
Perhaps some people will say that it is reasonable that the society is now in a relatively stable stage since the modern scene was marked by tremendously and ever-accelerating change in the past few years .
But it is not the main reason in my opinion .
Just take my home country for an example .
The most serious problem which hampers the innovation process is that the technological base is relatively lower than the developed world .
Since industry innovation is a process with very high risk .
In other words , it runs a high risk of failure .
From the research report given by the government we can see that more than eighty percent of the companies involved in the research have some experience of breaking off or failure .
Because of the low technological base , the possibility of coming across with some bottlenecks in the innovation process is higher .
In this way , companies may not dare to pump money into the development of new products .
As we all know , enough funds are the basic guarantee for innovation .
Once the process is lacking in funds , there is little hope for the process to continue .
Thus , the money we have already pumped in just becomes sunk cost , which means the company losses .
Then , this becomes a vicious circle .
High risk means less money pumped in .
Less money leads to higher risk of failure .
Finally , fewer and fewer companies will be willing to go for the innovation process .
Another very important factor in my opinion is about the lack of researchers .
As is known to all , the key to technological innovation is talents , especially those high-quality compound talents .
But the fact is that the lack of researchers has become the 'bottleneck ' restrictions for most of the companies in China .
Statistics suggest that the R&D teams in companies tend to decrease in number .
Worse still , there are many companies which even do not have an R&D team yet .
Besides , the not-so-high salary can hardly attract high-quality talents to join the research team , which is also due to the lack of funds which I have mentioned before .
All these make it even more difficult for the innovation to bear fruit .
Anyway , we are living in such a world where everyone is down-to-earth .
People always tend to do things that will benefit themselves .
As a consequence , R&D may become the last choice for some talents .
All in all , it is the low level of technological base and the lack of excellent researchers that significantly hamper the whole process for innovation .
To improve the present situation , it is better for the companies to pump in more money to the process and attract more talents to contribute to the technology progress .
Additionally , companies should import the advanced ideas and skills from the developed world to improve the technological skill of themselves .
Only in this way can we expect a better environment for innovation and more significant scientific and technological progress .
Two obstacles on the path of technology development in China
Recently , along with the rapid and sound development of China 's economy , China has made world-acknowledged achievements in technological area .
However , some obstacles still hamper the process of technological innovation in China , as the technological development is in its infancy .
In my opinion , there are mainly two big problems for us to overcome in the next few years , which are the decentralization and shortage of resources and the lagging of technology transfer .
Resource refers to human capital and financial support .
Actually , decentralization of human capital and delayed financial support hamper the stage of research and development to quite a large extent .
Compared to other developing countries , China does not have a standard innovation centre where many talented scientists and engineers can discuss and develop a creative project .
Take Silicon Valley as an example . Many new technological products are developed and promising companies are founded there every year , which is a result of the cooperation of many talents .
But China does not have such a base for technology development .
Chinese talents are spreading all over the world .
How to call them back and work out some big projects is a tough task for our government .
Lack of financial support is another problem faced by some existing research departments .
Every year , China raises a large quantity of money to support technology development .
However , if the financial support is divided into small parts , these departments will not receive enough money to do a large scale research .
Furthermore , some research projects must wait several months for financial support , during which time scientists may lose heart and give up .
As a result , research and development , which are the first steps of innovation , become more and more difficult .
China should try hard to solve the problem of resource .
Another problem is the lagging of technology transfer , which is a headache for a lot of research departments .
As we all know , technology was born to meet human 's needs .
Therefore , transforming technology into human lives is significant for an innovation .
Some new technologies may be born as well as die in laboratories as a result of lag in technology transfer .
Take computer as an example : it is said that the development speed of the computer is quite fast .
Perhaps it takes about a few years to generate a new generation .
But can most of us change our computers every few years ?
Impossible !
Besides , as China does not have a system to link these new techniques with manufacturing departments , I think most manufacturing factories can not make full use of these innovations .
Therefore , we had better build a sound system to do the technology transfer .
As far as I am concerned , the two problems are crucial as one refers to the beginning of innovation and the other determines the destiny of this innovation .
I think there are some ways to solve these problems .
Firstly , we had better develop some technical centre to bring talents together and do research .
Secondly , we must develop a complete system for technology transformation .
If we can overcome these problems , I believe the design process of innovation in China will develop farther more .
Two Problems hampering China 's engineering design processes for innovation
Nowadays , innovative engineering design plays a significant role in technology development .
A smart design , sometimes only involving a little change , can improve the work efficiency by several times , which can produce a much more attractive profit .
Obviously , cultivating engineers ' innovative abilities is very important .
However , in my home country , many problems exist as huge barriers for engineers to apply their innovative ideas to practice .
The improper education model and the unsatisfying experimental conditions stand out among many reasons .
First comes the improper education model in China .
Chinese students are very diligent , but sometimes they focus too much on theoretical knowledge and the exams , while overlooking the importance of cultivating their own innovative ability .
Many engineering students only care about passing exams , believing passing exam is everything .
So when they become engineers , what they can do is only follow others ' steps and they can not put forward new ideas .
Besides the cognition problem , some universities do not give much attention to students ' hands-on abilities .
Experiment classes , which aim to help students develop their application and innovative ability , are often deleted from the schedule or are conducted without experimental materials .
For technology , personal experience is the best way to learn .
Being taught in this way , many students can analyze theoretical questions deeply but can do nothing about practical problems .
Furthermore , we know it is hard for a person to come up with new ideas continuously but this process becomes much easier when several people exchange their thoughts about this issue .
This is the function of communication .
But teamwork sometimes is also overlooked .
If there is no project , no lab report , no design assignments , how can students learn to work with others ?
Hence , some engineers can not communicate ; they do not know how to allocate workload , and they do not know how to deal with difficulties and conflicts .
This greatly increases the difficulties to be innovative .
Another obstacle is the unsatisfactory social conditions for engineers to develop their knowledge and thoughts .
When I compare my hometown university engineering graduates with their peers in NUS , I find those from NUS have more opportunities to have internships in institutes , to do experiments in teams , to communicate with researchers and professors .
This is the problem of experimental condition .
Apart from this , China does not give as much attention to technology innovations as many other countries .
For example , in Singapore , there are many awards for engineers in various engineering fields while there are only a few which are quite hard to achieve .
Some engineers can not do experiments only because they can not get enough money .
This is the problem of financial condition .
Besides the above two problems , lack of communication in engineering society also discourages the generation of innovative ideas .
There are not many conferences on engineering topics which can allow engineers and scientists to exchange ideas and learn from each other .
This is the problem of communication condition .
After all the above , we can see the improper model and the unsatisfactory social conditions really act as obstacles to innovative engineering designs .
This may be a reason that China 's technology is still behind many countries in certain fields .
However , we must also see things are changing .
As China is carrying out education reform in a large scale and putting more emphasis on technology innovations , we can predict China 's technology has a good prospect .
DC Technology and the effect of consumers ' demand
In the olden days , taking photos was a complicated process performed solely by professional technicians .
A photographer had to adjust all the settings manually and develop the films with chemicals called film developer in a darkroom .
However , the recent two decades have witnessed an astonishing innovation in photography . The development of digital cameras ( DC ) has made taking photos accessible to the non-specialists .
This development came as a result of people 's demand for more convenient photo-taking techniques .
Consumers ' desire for more convenient usagof the camera and better photo quality shapes the development of the DC technology .
Consumers ' desire for the convenient usage of the camera includethe simplicity of camera functions and the accessibility of photo sharing .
Both of these requests stimulate the DC technology development in respective ways .
Since most non-specialists do n't possess sufficient photography knowledge , developing user-friendly functions is essential to enable these people to take good photos .
Innovations like the face detection technology by Sony Company , was developed in response to the increasing popularity of portrait taking ( Cheung , K. M. , 2007 ) .
Other technologies like the anti-blink function and the Smile Shutter function , which automatically capture the best facial expressions , are also developed in the need for simpler photo taking ( Sony Unveils World 's First Wi-Fi Digital Camera with Web Browser , 2009 ) .
Photo sharing ability is another feature consumers value .
In the past , photos could only be printed on paper , which confined the sharing of photos .
However , modern DC technology effectively solves this problem by involving web access .
Users can upload photos onto the Internet and share the photos with more people .
A case in point is the Wi-Fi cameras .
With a web browser installed within it , this camera can get access to any hotspot , allowing users to upload their photos immediately after shooting ( Sony Unveils World 's First Wi-Fi Digital Camera with Web Browser , 2009 ) .
Another development that improved the convenience of taking photos is portability .
This is achieved by reducing the size of a DC .
The technology of compact DC has made this development particularly prominent .
These DCs are generally less than 20mm thick , with a weight of approximately 150 grams .
Thus , they can be put inside pockets and easily carried around .
The improvement in portability has also resulted from people 's increasing demand for easy casual shootings .
Consumers ' pursuit of better photo quality has also driven the innovation of advanced DC technology .
The quality of a photo is mainly determined by the resolution of a camera .
To improve resolution , a company needs to conduct a considerable amount of research which included the generation of new ideas , technology breakthroughs and repetitive experiments .
The process is hard , but companies are willing to sacrifice their time and labor for the development . This is because the better the resolution , the more customers will be attracted , and the more profit the companies will make .
Original digital cameras had a very low resolution .
For example , the first commercial DC , Dycam Model 1 , provided only 90,000 pixels ( Kamps , 2008 ) .
After half a decade , the number changed to 1,000,000 .
The most advanced DCs today support more than 12 million pixels , which is over 100 times that of the original ones .
Such remarkable improvement shows the strong impact of consumers ' requirement in pushing forward the DC technology .
The innovation and development of the DC technology are shaped by the needs of the market .
Consumers ' desire for better photo-taking experience stimulates companies to develop their technology and provide better products to compete for customers .
At the same time , the technological developments have greatly changed our lives at the same time , making our lives more convenient and colourful .
