In reality , there are many problems faced by researchers when they are developing innovations .
These problems may occur at various stages of the innovation process ( Rogers , 2003 ) .
In this essay , we will discuss an innovation of a Bluetooth communication system for cyclists , one of the problems that the Bluetooth innovation faces and the impacts the problem has on certain stages of the process .
This Bluetooth communication system ( bike bug ) encounters the challenge of a lack of market demand which affects the commercialization process of it launched into the market .
There are many sub-factors that cause the shortage of market demand .
First , it is the usefulness of bike bug to cyclists .
Although , the number of cyclists who ride in large groups has risen over the years , the number of official members of " joyriders " stood at 120 in May 2007 ( JoyRiders , 2006 ) . Compared to Singapore 's population of 4.84 million people in June 2008 ( Singapore Government , 1871 ) , the number of cyclists that cycle in groups are insignificant .
Therefore , this indicates that the demand is low as cyclists who travel alone , in pairs , or in small groups and those people who do not cycle do not require nor have the need for the bike plural .
Second , it is the design of the bike bug .
The structure of the bike bug 's earpiece does not fit snuggly into the cyclist 's ear .
Therefore , there is the possibility of the cyclist incurring extra cost to replace the lost earpiece , especially after the earpiece is drenched by the cyclist 's perspiration .
The extra replacement cost is incurred because if the cyclist speeds , the moist earpiece with minimal grip is likely to drop off .
Third , it is the acceptance level of consumers .
Although , hands-free and Bluetooth devices for headsets are in abundance in the current market , its sales record is not so promising as compared to other Bluetooth gadgets for mobile phone , cars , and computers .
Even though 33 million of Bluetooth headsets were shipped in , the figure is expected to increase to over 55 million in 2006 ( Bluetooth special interest group , 2006 ) . Compared to the world population of 6,808,515,373 people ( Geohive , 2009 ) , who use Bluetooth , this is considered the minority .
This denotes that Bluetooth headsets are still not used by the majority .
Hence , as the bike bug is a Bluetooth headset gadget , I predict a dim future for its sales record .
With this two sub-factors plus the slow acceptance of Bluetooth headset compared to other Bluetooth devices , potential manufacturers of the bike bug are deterred as it is not economically practical to mass produce the bike bikebugs .
It is therefore shown that for bike bugs to be successfully launched into society , much is needed to raise the bike bug 's popularity among the public .
