Title : Two Problems that Significantly Hamper Engineering Design Processes For Innovations and Their Effects .
Who are the designers of all the inventions in this world ?
Well , obviously they are engineers .
" Scientists dream about doing great things .
Engineers do them .
" This is a famous quote by James A. Michener .
Engineers are people who investigate problems and try to solve them .
Gadgets and machines were invented to solve our daily problems .
However , some obstacles may obstruct the innovative engineering design processes .
Identifying these obstacles and their effects may help to prevent rejection of inventions in the future .
The educational system in Malaysia is exam orientated .
The educational system is based more towards theory and not practicel .
Students spend most of their time in the classroom learning theory .
The amount of time spent in labs for practicals is minimal .
The system is giving emphasis on memory skills , not analytical skills .
Furthermore , school teachers merely teach students to pass their exams , but not for the students to learn .
Primary and secondary school students are spoon fed with information rather than letting them learning independently .
Students are taught and train to score in public examinations such as Penilaian Menengah Rendah ( PMR ) and Sijil Pelajaran Malaysia ( SPM ) .
Eventually , students with good memory will be able to pass with flying colours as they are able to memorise all the facts and spit them all out during the exams .
These excellent students will face problems as they further their studies to be engineers .
They lack of analytical skills , creativity and innovation .
These are the two factors that will cease engineers from being able to solve real life problems .
It is human 's nature to response to incentives .
Incentives could be in the form of money or moral support .
Society does not show an encouraging attitude towards innovation .
Society is afraid to accept new invention .
This causes engineers to be demotivated .
In conjunction to this , companies are not willing to take risks to fund the research and development of engineers .
For example , Tetsuya Mizoguchi , a brilliant engineer who envisioned and invented the laptop .
He idea was rejected by Toshiba fearing that it will not be successful [ 1 ] .
This scenario is common and it is preventing engineers from doing research and development for a given problem .
Research and development is almost impossible without funding .
Thus , engineers are not capable to come up with solution to problems although they have brilliant ideas .
In conclusion , our current educational system is too rigid .
It should focus more on practical applications rather than theory .
Students must be exposed to new problems related to current issues and be able to propose solutions to the problem .
In addition , the solutions must be practical and appropriate to be accepted by society .
Nevertheless , society should be open to fresh ideas .
Positive feedback from society will stimulate passion and desire for engineers to contribute with what they do best , inventing .
[ 1 ] Rogers , E.M. ( 2003 ) .
The birth of the laptop computer at Toshiba .
In Diffusion of innovations ( pp.144- 146 ) .
New York : Free Press .
Factors Influence the Adoption of Car
Since the birth of cars at the end of the last century , its development has gone through ups and downs over the last one hundred years .
The first car invented by Carl .
Zaochu Benz at that time only had a speed of 18Km per hour , but now , a sports car can accelerate from zero speed to 100Km per hour within 3 seconds .
We can see that the speed of development of cars is very amazing .
But what actually made the car industry develop so fast ?
The car industry ' development can not be separated from the adoption of cars by the people .
Only the customers ' demand can stimulate the enhancement of the car industry .
So again , what are the factors influencing the adoption of cars ?
Actually , after Henry Ford 's Model T automobile was invented , cars just gradually became a vehicle that can be adopted by people .
The { Model T } was introduced on October 1 , 1908 .
The car was very simple to drive , and easy and cheap to repair .
Because the Model T was so cheap by the 1920s a majority of American drivers learned to drive on the Model T. Apart from this , in order to make the Model T more universal , Henry Ford also created a massive publicity machine in Detroit to ensure every newspaper carried stories and ads about the new product .
He also created a network of local dealers , which provided a good marketing channel for his company .
These are all the things that the Ford Company did in order to sell more Model Ts .
If we analyse from a broader view , we can see that there were also other economical factors influence the cars ' adoption .
One of the most important factors was the declining cost of oil at that time .
Several years before Ford produced the Model T , in 1901 , the discovery of Spindletop really means a new age of oil mass production .
The daily output of this site was more than 1 million barrels of oil , which was even more than the sum of output of other sites in the United States .
This made the price of oil in the United States decrease very fast at that time .
So the lower price of oil provided a good background for the adoption of Ford 's Model T .
But the Model T was just the beginning of the adoption of cars , another factor which made the technology of producing cars became more mature was the military demand .
In 1941 , the World War 1 broke out ; in 1939 , the World War 2 broke out .
Many countries were involved in these wars .
During the wars , vehicles and armored transportation became essential for every country .
This caused a big demand for the engineers , designers and machinists .
Many new technologies were invented during the wars .
For example , a very popular hydraulic actuator which is still used today was developed in the early part of World War 2 .
All these inventions not only increased the usefulness time of the engine , but also improved the overall situation of the car industry .
Government policies and priorities also play an important role in the development of the car industry .
After World War 2 , the United States became the most developed country in the world .
After the United States won the war , many ordnance factories became civil factories ; this made the expansion of the car industry faster .
With the developing of the word 's economics , the car industry had already become a very important standard to measure a country 's development level .
So the government provided the priority to the car industry .
Especially during the Cold War , in order to demonstrate America 's superiority and wealth , the government encouraged the car industry .
In conclusion , the adoption of cars and this technological innovation was not only technological determined , but also shaped by economic factors , military demands , and government policies and priorities .
Two problems hamper engineering design processes for innovations in China and their effects .
An engineering design process , which includes mainly five stages-defining the problem , gathering pertinent information , generating multiple solutions , analyzing and selecting a solution and testing and implementing the solution , plays a very important role in improving the quality of people 's lives .
In our daily life , we can easily identify automobiles , laptops , handphones and microwaves as engineering innovations around us .
Take China for instance , the culture for innovation in engineering design is severely lacking .
One factor would be China 's rigid education system that produces only a small number of innovative engineers .
It does not help either that the Chinese government is giving very little incentives for innovations in engineering .
As a result , engineers have little motivation to innovate .
Even if they have the motivation , their ideas are unlikely to come true due to the lack of funding from the government .
One of the problems that hampers innovations in engineering may be China 's rigid education system which discourages students from being creative .
In China , most teachers spoon-feed their students , and students are instructed to follow standard answers given by the teachers .
When a student finds out an alternative method of solving a problem , there is usually no reward for him and he still has to answer the question in the standard way to avoid losing marks at examinations .
Thus innovation among students is discouraged .
Moreover , students have very little hands-on practice of what they have learnt in class .
Instead , most of their time is spent on solving sample examination problems .
As a result , China 's education system produces engineers that are generally weak in doing research and development , which will adversely affect the stage of generating multiple solutions in the engineering design process .
Another problem that poses obstacles to innovation in engineering in China may be the lack of funds from the government for research and development ( R&D ) , thus engineers are hardly attracted to the R&D industry and the lack of funds to implement their new ideas .
Furthermore , the government gives very little reward to engineers who have done innovative work .
Mr. Yuan Long Ping , who is known as the " Father of Hybrid Rice " , had contributed to tremendous increase in the level of world 's rice output through cultivating species of hybrid rice during the 1970 's .
His innovation has saved millions of people from hunger all over the world .
However , the Chinese government only awarded him the State Preeminent Science and Technology Award of China in 2000 which was valued at merely 5 million yuan .
This was very little if it was meant to reward him for his more than twenty years ' of hard work .
This lack of government support means , engineers will not be able to get resources , raw materials , precise equipment for research during engineering design process .
Thus the stage of testing and implementing solutions during the engineering design process is greatly hampered
In conclusion , the rigid education system and the governments limited incentives for innovations limit the number of innovative engineers and hinder the realization of innovative ideas if any .
These are hence two prominent obstacles that hamper the engineering design processes for innovations in China .
