There have been many controversial debates on whether the government should limit public spending on the elderly so that money can be diverted to other areas of the country 's development .
While the latter is important and inevitable , our government is being challenged to cope with the need to increase allocation of public funds for the elderly with a graying population .
Rising living expenses and healthcare costs is also another factor which highlights the importance of limiting public spending on the elderly .
If a limit is not in place , the government might soon face difficulties in funding other key sectors of the country 's development such as education and defense .
Smaller side effects include the over reliance on the government to support the aged .
This will turn the regime of supporting the elderly into a nightmare and burden to the society .
Firstly , with a graying population , the government is expected to spend more public funds each year on areas like healthcare for the elderly and elderly friendly public facilities such as slopes in housing estates .
This section of public expenditure soaks up a large portion of the government 's annual allocated budget each year .
In Singapore 's 2009 budget allocation , a total of $ 66 million ( up from a total of $ 55.6 million in the year 2008 ) was allocated from the pool of $ 18.1 billion allocated for social development .
This spending allocation only covers enhancements for the elderly in the society and excludes healthcare benefits coverage .
This expenditure is expected to rise further with the increase of life expectancy and the increase in the proportion of the elderly ( from 8.6 % in 2008 to 18.7 % in 2030 ) .
To quote a statistic from Singapore 's 2008 budget day report , in 2007 , 21 % of the Government Healthcare Expenditure ( GHE ) , or 0.2 % of the country 's GDP was dedicated to elderly care .
This proportion of spending is expected to double in the next three decades .
Secondly , the rise of living expenses and healthcare costs continuously is another reason why should government limit public spending for this sector .
With this , more public funds will have to be allocated to fund this sector .
This scenario would only be ideal should our economy achieve constant growth in economy in the upcoming years and the spending on other sectors remain unchanged .
However , it is increasingly difficult to forecast such growth with the current volatile and gloomy economy worldwide .
With this situation , other key sectors such as education and defense might be implicated when the targeted Gross Domestic Product ( GDP ) to sustain the increased expense on the aged is not met , should a spending limit on the aged not be imposed .
This might in turn lead to a series of negative chain effects such as making education less affordable .
Thirdly , the constant aid from the government might lead to unsightly social problem .
Unfilial children might abuse the system by pushing their responsibilities of supporting their parents to the government .
Also , young adults might not also see the importance of savings during their financially active years since the government will do their part in providing subsidy for the needy .
Many people might however , disagree that government should limit the public spending on aged .
This view is valid as well .
If the government is to limit public spending on this sector and instead channel it to other sectors , this might deprive the needy elderly from obtaining assistance and might lead to adverse effects .
Some of the adverse effects includes , elderly people especially those who are childless , spouseless and elderly people who does not have huge amount of savings to face difficulties to fund their own healthcare or living expenses .
This might in turn result in a social problem where an elderly might be utilized by crime syndicates to commit crimes to fund those expenses .
To deal with these challenges , the government has limited options , and one of which is to limit the spending on this sector to reduce the burden on the society .
One such way is to look into a tiered system when providing healthcare subsidies for the elderly .
Alternatively , government can also look at ways to further enhance the current Central Provident Fund ( CPF ) , MediSave and MediSheild schemes to enforce compulsory savings during one 's financial active years to prepare them for old age .
Filial piety should also be enforced .
There should be a limit to how much the government should spent on the elderly group .
The issue if not dealt with properly , might place government into a challenging position , lack of funds on important sectors such as education and defence and also causing the citizens to be over reliant on the society .
These will all turn the regime into a nightmare .
