Sodium-cooled Fast Reactor , the Future Plant in Use
Every nation is caught up with the energy shortage problem .
If man continues exhausting natural resources at the present rate , the day is not far off when human beings may run out of coal or oil .
Nuclear power stands out as one of the most prospective alternate sources .
In the case of nuclear power , the allure is clear : abundant power , no carbon dioxide emissions , no blots on the landscape except an occasional containment dome and cooling tower , according to Michael P. ( 2005 ) .
However , problems come with nuclear power such as accidents of leakage , waste reprocessing and storage , high construction costs , and even the possible use of nuclear fuel for weapons .
With considerable research background , Sodium-cooled Fast Reactors , or SFR , are able to ease public worry over these safety issues as mentioned before .
The most serious concern raised by many is the possibility of accidents .
In the event of leakage , numerous people will probably be left dead , much more affected in a long lasting period henceforth .
People can still recall " on Mar .
28 , 1979 , the Three Mile Island nuclear power plant experienced a partial meltdown .
Cleanup of the accident required $ 1 billion and longer than 10 years to complete , " as repoted in findingDulcinea Staff ( 2009 ) .
A community of misfortunes have happened , mainly resulting from a failure in control of pressure and temperature .
Chance for SFR to get out of control is relatively smaller because SFR " operates near atmospheric pressure , and intermediate sodium system between the radioactive sodium in the primary system and the power conversion system " as revealed by Generation IV International Forum .
In contrast , the other five designs of reactors , which are Gas-cooled fast reactors , Lead-cooled fast reactors , Molten salt reactors , Supercritical-water-cooled reactors , and Very-high-temperature reactors , operate either at high temperature or under high pressure or even both .
Besides , double-wall piping and high reliable SG with double-wall tube are designed for prevention of likely sodium chemical reactions .
If all these back-up systems go wrong , SFR still can pull through safely as the passive reactor shutdown system and decay heat removal by natural circulation are still in position as a line of defence .
People may also tremble at the long-lived radioactive waste .
The waste , if not handled carefully , may pollute the atmosphere and water and remain there for long .
Moreover , conventional reprocessing recovers plutonium and uranium from waste , as worried in Plutonium Wars ( 2004 ) .
The proliferation , an essential step of reprocessing , does generate weapon-grade plutonium .
However , the anxiety can be eased by SFR due to its non-proliferation attributes .
Generation IV International Forum reported that " the envisaged SFR capability to efficiently and nearly completely consume trans-uranium as fuel would reduce the actinide loadings in the high-level radioactive waste it produces .
Such reductions would bring benefits in the radioactive waste disposal requirements associated with the system and enhance its non-proliferation attributes .
" The research on waste disposal and reprocessing is still going on for the envisaged SFR to be realized .
The final objection raised by opponents is the high construction cost of SFR .
I agree that it is a major challenge for SFR .
However , it can be offset by a more efficient utilization of fuel resources .
Generation IV International Forum remarked that the fuel cycle employs a full actinide recycle with three major options .
The options range from a large size ( 600 to 1,500 Mwe ) to a small one ( 50 to 150 MWe ) .
The fuel cycles employed also differ in the reactors of the three scales .
Customers can choose to bear different costs according to their own needs .
This reduces their potential cost in the construction of a reactor which is larger than they need .
The SFR 's fast spectrum also makes it possible to use available fissile and fertile materials ( including depleted uranium ) considerably more efficiently than thermal spectrum reactors with once-through fuel cycles , as reported by Generation IV International Forum .
Such reduction in fuel cost solves the problem noted by MIT ( 2003 ) that " economically , new nuclear plants would not be able to compete with coal and gas .
" Apart from benefit from considerable technological experience , SFR also offers the potential to operate with a high conversion fast spectrum core , with the resulting benefit of increasing the utilization of fuel resources as Generation IV International Forum highlighted .
It should be evident that none of the opposing statements is valid for SFR .
SFR operates at low temperature and low pressure and has multiple systems to back up safety .
The radioactive waste can also be sustainably used in the fuel cycle with reduction of environmental burden .
High construction cost is offset not only by multiple construction options , but also by efficient utilization of fuel resources .
More importantly , research is going on for SFR , the most promising generation IV reactor .
