Using Psychology during Design Process Helps Prevent Major Accidents
Since the beginning of the 20th century , a number of major engineering accidents have occurred with increasing complexity of large engineering projects .
It is important to study these cases and analyze the causes in order to prevent potential accidents .
This essay will bring one particular accident , the Eschede train disaster , back to the table .
The ICE train accident in Eschede happened in June 1998 .
It caused 101 deaths and injured 88 , which made it the worst accident in the history of Germany ( BBC News , 1998 ) .
The main factors that lead to the disaster include the failure in wheel and train track design , as well as an inefficient feedback system .
This essay will propose that the use of psychology during the design process will improve product safety and it might have helped prevent the Eschede train disaster .
First of all , if psychology approaches were introduced in the design process , product safety could be better guaranteed .
It is very often that managers of large projects tend to believe that engineers would never make mistakes .
However , the fact is human errors commonly exist in engineering designs process , which may result in the failure of product functions ( Wilbert , 2007 ) .
In the case of Eschede train disaster , the newly designed high-speed wheels were used for operation without being physically tested ( HYPERLINK " http : //www.ndt.net/index.html " NDTnet , 2000 ) .
A single fatigue crack later contributed the most to the train accident .
It reveals the fact that engineers sometime neglect certain steps of design process .
If the wheels were tested and improved before they were put to service , the accident would have likely been prevented .
Therefore , a human scientist is in need to analyze the design process and ensure every step is completed before the product is commissioned .
In addition , human scientist will focus on factors that are closely related to the design , such as the environment in which the product will be implemented .
Companies hire engineers to design a certain product and they expect them to consider every detail related to the design .
But in reality , engineers often focus on the product itself while ignoring the environment .
In the case of high-speed train , the track is important to analyze .
The limit of the track , upon which the ICE train was travelling , was exceeded by the train 's maximum speed .
Besides , when travelling from high to low speed track , the train passes through switch points which caused one carriage to stray from the straight-line .
It is analyzed by scientist that it is likely that the train could have passed safely under the bridge if the track was plain ( ICE High Speed Train Disaster , n.d. ) .
Suppose a human scientist had been present during the design process , he probably would have reminded the engineers to consider the track and the accident would have been prevented .
Furthermore , psychologists can help analyze and improve the operating system and company policy of large engineering projects .
Just before the Eschede train disaster occurred , valuable time was lost due to inefficient feedback system .
The train staff did not stop and examine the problem when a passenger reported strange noise and a large piece of metal coming up through the floor .
Engineers and project manager may not be experts in designing operation rules .
A human scientist can assist them and improve the system .
If the " stop and examine " rule had been suggested before the accident for dealing with danger signals , the disaster could have been prevented ( Single Line Tablet Instruments , n.d. ) .
To conclude , using psychology approaches during design process will help improve product safety as well as the efficiency of operating systems .
Human scientists are able to analyze each step of the process and remind engineers of the important factors that they neglect .
Many major accidents might be prevented if psychology is involved .
Therefore , it is vital to introduce psychology into engineering design process .
