The two general IV nuclear reactors that we will be discussing are very high temperature reactor ( VHTR ) and gas cooled fast reactor ( GCFR ) .
I personally feel that VHTR deserves more funding and research than GCFR in terms of performance , safety and cost .
The similarities between these two reactors are that both use helium as a coolant , do not need working at high pressure and produce the same output of hydrogen and electricity .
( Butler 2004 ) .
We will be considering which reactor should have more emphasis to have further research and development funding for it .
In terms of performance , Gas cooled fast reactors are dependent on VHTR in terms of modification in design and characteristic , minimizing the effort required for GCFR .
( www.nextgenerationnuclearplant.com ) .
The waste material produced by GCFR is being recycled .
As a result , there is a decrease in efficiency as it is being used many times and loses its capability to generate the same amount of energy .
( http : //www.neutron.kth.se/courses/GenIV/GenIVreactors.pdf ) .
In addition , according to theory , we know that output efficiency is dependent on temperature .
So a higher operating temperature of VHTR would allow for more electricity to be produced compared to gas cooled reactors .
Besides that , the waste material being produced by gas cooled fast reactors is pure plutonium and this material is mainly used for weapon production .
This is dangerous if it goes out of hand .
In terms of safety or security , there is a temperature monitoring system in VHTR to ensure safety .
It monitors the reactors to avoid any meltdown .
Such system does not exist in gas cooled reactors that operate at lower temperature .
( http : //www.cogeneration.net/very_high_temperature_Reactor.htm ) .
VHTR has a better back-up system .
In the event of sudden shutdown due to malfunction , VHTR can still produce energy .
( http : //www.anl.gov/PCS/acsfuel ) .
It is especially useful for countries like France , where 80 % of the electricity generated is dependent on nuclear energy .
( Butler 2004 ) .
For GCFR , there is a presence of safe accommodation of low thermal inertia and poor heat transfer properties of the coolant .
In VHTR , waste is not recycled but instead stored away .
In terms of cost , VHTR can be implemented five years earlier than the gas cooled fast reactors .
As a result , this can save money for many years of project planning and research .
( Butler 2004 ) Currently , there are no operators for the GCFR in the market .
Money will need to be invested in the training of operators for this new type of generation IV reactors .
( www.ostigov/bridge/servlets/pur/840501-9gpzj3/nature840501.pdf ( 3 and 4 ) .
In conclusion , I personally feel that VHTR does deserve more research and funding in it than gas cooled fast reactors .
This is because it is better in terms of performance , cost and safety .
