Gas Cooled Fast Reactor
Introduction
The Chernobyl accident that happened on 26 April 1986 , has led everyone to realize the damages and consequences that we might face when we build a nuclear power plant .
[ Greenpeace International , 2009 ] Nonetheless , the Chernobyl accident was an outcome due to an old technology and poor management in the past .
In the next 50 years , demand for energy would definitely double with rapidly growing technology and production [ Michelangelo Network , 2008 ] .
With present day technologies , the Generation IV International Forum has proposed six new and advance nuclear reactors .
The six nuclear reactors are not only sustainable and economically viable solutions ; they are also a safe and reliable source of energy .
Among the six concepts of nuclear reactors , the Very High Temperature Reactor , ( VHTR ) and the Gas Cooled Fast Reactor , ( GCFR ) are quite similar , but the Gas Cooled Fast Reactor has more benefits than VHTR in terms of performance , sustainability and cost .
Performance
VHTR is a high performance reactor which uses helium to cool the heating components during operation .
VHTR uses a mixed fuel composition to operate and this composition can produce improvement in fuel performance thus making it a very effective reactor in generating electricity .
This composition is a mixture of 15 % carbide and 85 % oxide in the fissile particles .
[ GCFR is a concept which uses the reference design of VHTR , thus it is also a reactor of high performance .
Since GCFR operates at 850 degrees Celsius , there are more choices of materials that can be used in the construction of GCFR .
[ Idaho National Laboratory , 2002 ] Because GCFR operates at a lower temperature as compared to the VTHR , therefore the materials used for construction is cheaper and there are more choices of material available .
Sustainability
GCFR is an excellent choice in terms of sustainability because it is capable in minimizing radioactive waste more effectively as compared to the VHTR .
The nuclear waste of the GCFR is able to be put back into the process multiple times , and this reduces the nuclear waste produced .
[ , 2009 ] The reduction of radioactive waste uses processes called pryo-chemical or hydrometallurgical .
[ Commissariat à l'energie atomique , 2007 ] With this there is a great reduction of nuclear waste , and money can be saved to build a storage site for nuclear waste .
Therefore , GCFR not only can have efficient energy conversion with the current design but also has an economically beneficial outcome .
[ Idaho National Laboratory , 2002 ]
Cost
The cost for building a VHTR prototype would cost US $ 1.1 billion .
However for GCFR , the total ten year budget of GCFR is US $ 940 million and it includes the cost from planning to completion of the reactor .
However this is shared by all eight Generation IV International Forum members .
Therefore the annual budget for each country is US $ 7-8 million .
In this budget , the required sum of construction cost is US $ 300 million .
[ Idaho National Laboratory , 2002 ]
Conclusion
In conclusion , although the there are several advantages of the VHTR however there are factors that makes it difficult to implement it as it is more expensive and limited by choices of construction materials as compared with the GCFR .
I would give my fullest support for the concept of GCFR among the six concepts proposed in the Generation IV International forum .
Nonetheless , the decision to implement the reactors still falls in the hands of the countries that are highly interested as the budget for every country is different .
[ Cogeneration TechnologiesTM , 2002 ]
