The Nuclear Funding Controversy
Nuclear technology has experienced several revolutions in the past fifty years .
The nuclear reactors currently in operation are Generation II and III reactors .
Generation II reactors were first developed in the 1950s , and were widely adopted in the following 40 years .
Generation III reactors , invented in the 1990s , are the most advanced in use nowadays ( Wallenius , n.d. ) .
However , after the concept of Generation IV reactors was raised by GIF in 2001 ( Butler , 2004 ) , whether governments should solely focus on current nuclear reactors or split the funding for the new generation became a controversy .
In my opinion , governments should fund the Gen IV reactors , as they possess remarkable advantages in terms of safety , performance and costs relative to the old designs .
Some people claim that Gen II and Gen III reactors are safe enough for operation .
Despite the accident in Chernobly , Ukraine , which was mainly caused by man-made operating errors ( Chernobly accident , 2009 ) , no major accident has occurred in the past two decades .
Rather than fund the Gen IV reactors , they think that governments should use the money to reinforce the safety of currently operating reactors .
However , malfunctions that had the risk of being disastrous happened from time to time .
For instance , in 2006 , part of the Forsmark-1 reactor in Sweden failed to start , almost resulting in a catastrophic meltdown .
A Belgian reactor experienced a similar problem in the preceding year ( Nuclear reactor safety , 2007 ) .
Such incidents imply that the potential risks of the Gen II and Gen III reactors can not be ignored .
The Gen IV reactors can significantly change this risky situation .
Take the VHTR reactor as an example .
In this new design , the fuel pellets used in nuclear reaction are fused in graphite pebbles and coated with ceramic layers which effectively prevent the radioactive materials from leaking .
In other words , no radioactive damage will occur even if there is a meltdown ( Butler , 2004 ) .
Therefore , the Gen IV reactors are more reliable than the previous generations .
In order to achieve safer operating environment , government should fund the development of the Gen IV .
Opponents also argue that the Gen II and Gen III reactors have already achieved excellent performance , so it is not necessary to develop the new generation .
As a successful example , France obtains over 75 % of its electricity from nuclear power .
The low cost associated with nuclear energy has made France the largest electricity exporter .
Though the result appears favorable , this performance is only based on the short term effects .
Other factors should be included for a more objective estimation .
Gen II and III reactors produce high level radioactive wastes at a rate of 12000 tons per year around the globe .
These wastes take thousands of years to lose their activity . Thus they are not environmentally friendly in the long term ( Radioactive wastes , 2001 ) .
Moreover , the storage of the wastes is both money and labor consuming .
A repository built to store the wastes could cost up to $ 90 billion , like that in Yucca Mountain , US ( Tetreault , 2008 ) .
However , these problems can be solved using the Gen IV technology .
In one of its designs , VHTR , the used fuel pebbles can be automatically selected , and those with some life left are recycled ( Butler , 2004 ) .
This process reduces the amount as well as the radioactive level of the final wastes .
Therefore , it will lead to more sustainable performance in the long term .
This advantage proves that it is worthwhile to fund the Gen IV technology .
Opponents also suggest that Gen IV reactors have no commercial benefits .
For example , building and testing a Gen IV nuclear plant costs about US $ 1 billion , which is a considerable investment ( Butler , 2004 ) .
On the other hand , the cost of generating electricity from current nuclear reactors is already quite low ( 2.3 pence per kilowatt ) , which is comparable with other major sources ( The cost of generating electricity , 2004 ) .
As a result , it is not worth spending additional money funding the new designs .
However , a typical Gen II reactor can only operate at an efficiency of around 30 % , meaning that a large proportion of the energy is wasted .
But with the technology of Gen IV reactors , the efficiency can be improved 45 % to 50 % due to extremely high operating temperature ( 1000 degrees Celsius ) ( Daniels , 2007 ) .
Numerically , this increase in efficiency can be reflected by a cut in costs of approximately 30 % for the same amount of electricity generated .
Moreover , the heat produced by the Gen IV reactors can be used to produce hydrogen from water or supplied to the petrochemical industry ( The very-high-temperature reactor , n.d. ) .
The cogeneration and applications make the total efficiency even higher , thus further reducing the operating costs .
From these statistics , it is obvious that Gen IV reactors are more profitable in the long run compared to the old generations .
Thus , Gen IV reactors should be funded by the government .
Though the Generation II and III reactors have been maturely adopted in electricity generation , the drawbacks in their designs largely confine the potential power of nuclear energy .
In order to optimize its power to meet the increasing demand of electricity as well as to maintain long term sustainability , governments should fund the Generation IV reactors instead of the old generation ones .
