Lead Cooled Fast Reactor Versus Sodium Cooled Fast Reactor
One of the Achilles ' heels of nuclear power plants is the danger of radioactive radiation leakage to the environment .
Areas around such power plants become environmentally hostile to human and wildlife health .
Unfortunately for nuclear power plants , the Chernobyl nuclear incident has convinced the public to not support nuclear plants .
However , the government is pushing for a new generation IV technology that is safer and ensures that the Chernobyl event does not happen again .
Generation IV nuclear reactor plants promise higher redundancy , higher efficiency , and better safety features than its predecessors .
The main objective in this new technological research is to search for cost effective and sustainable solutions to nuclear power ( Hejzlar,2005 ) .
In this essay , I shall discuss the advantage of Lead cooled Fast Reactor ( LFR ) over Sodium cooled Fast Reactor ( SFR ) on the aspects of safety , cost and performance .
The LFR has better safety features compared to SFR .
The element sodium has a low boiling point of 1156 Kelvin , compared to lead 's higher 2023 Kelvin , which is dangerous because it becomes overheated and starts to boil ( Hejzlar , 2009 ) .
In the event of an unprotected total loss of power , the LFR 's core is able to passively quickly cool down , due to its low pressure drop and simple coolant design ( Tucek , 2005 ) .
Compared to the SFR , it has a high pressure drop and thus its temperature would increase dangerously beyond its boiling point .
Additional safety devices have to be implemented ( Hejzlar , 2009 ) .
On another note , the low reactivity and inertia of lead allows safe removal of fuel if the fuel rod pins fail during installation .
On the other hand , lead can become radioactive and is therefore harmful to life during refueling when the lead is exposed .
While research is being done to control the lead 's radioactivity , it is reassuring that refueling is done every 8 years in large plants and every 15 years in smaller plants .
The risk of radioactive leakage is therefore minimal over its span of usage .
Cost has become a very important criterion in the world today . In this case , the LFR fits the bill very well .
Considering the economies of scale , the cost of building materials such as steel and concrete will increase significantly , hence the size of a nuclear plant has become a consideration .
The LFR 's smaller core and lesser required cooling systems , such as the omission of an intermediate cooling link , saves significant building costs as compared to SFR .
In fact , the lead/bismuth-cooled SVBR-75/100 is the cheapest to build amongst other types of fast reactors .
( Hejzlar , 2009 ) Furthermore , it costs less per kWh of electricity compared to SFR .
Another strong advantage is the scalability of LFR that allows it to be used in developing and hard to reach places , for instance , Africa .
( Tucek , 2005 )
On the contrary , research on the use of pure lead instead of a lead-bismuth cooling agent may outweigh the economic benefits of LFR .
It is also important to note that if SFR 's research on the reactivity of sodium is successful , a better cost saving SFR power plant can be built ( Zhao , 2009 ) .
Also , the SFR is projected to be launched 10 years earlier than LFR .
For the time being , LFR has the advantage of costing less today .
The performance capability of LFR does not disappoint .
LFR do not require maintaining high pressures in the core unlike SFR .
Also LFR has better fuel efficiency than SFR as LFR has less specific power , 45kW/kgHM compared to 65kW/kgHM of the SFR .
( Tucek , 2005 ) .
On the other hand , SFR does have some good points .
Sodium is four times more effective as a cooling agent than LFR , because of its better thermal hydraulic properties .
( Tucek , 2005 ) .
Furthermore , SFR has better net efficiency than LFR , 46.2 % versus 34.6 % for the latter .
SFR has been the mainstream for nuclear power plants in the past years and is therefore more familiar and accepted .
However research is needed to prove the capability of sodium at high temperatures , as it has a low boiling point , which will result in a complex design .
All in all , the overwhelming advantages of LFR as a future implementation of the new generation IV nuclear plant , proves that it is better than the SFR in all of the above aspects .
Therefore , we should push for development of LFR nuclear power plants .
