The merits of further research into VHTR technology
The most common method of power generation for close to 150 years the burning of fossil fuels such as oil or coal .
However , the byproducts from the burning of fossil fuels , greenhouse and acidic gases , have caused much devastation to our planet .
Perhaps a solution to save our planet will be to use a cleaner energy source -- nuclear power , which does not emit any harmful gases .
The Very High Temperature Reactor ( VHTR ) is a Gen IV reactor that shows great promise and potential .
However , detractors of nuclear power cited the risk of another catastrophic accident , the proliferation of nuclear arms , the lower thermal efficiency of nuclear plants and the high cost of building a plant .
This article will attempt to justify the merits of funding further research on VHTR through refuting the detractors ' claims in terms of safety , performance and cost .

The world is concerned with the safety of nuclear power plants and the possibility of nuclear arms proliferation . Past incidents at Chernobyl and Three Mile Island ( Bodansky , 2005 ) have left many in doubt about the technology .
Recently , , both North Korea and Iran have been told to step back on their plans of a nuclear power plant for fear that the same radioactive materials used for energy generation will be used in producing nuclear weapons .
The VHTR design does not include the controversial method of waste recycling , whereby plutonium is extracted in a pure form ( Butler , 2004 ) .
Hence , this design reduces the probability of nuclear arms proliferation .
Within the VHTR reactor , the nuclear fuel is enclosed within pebbles of silicon carbide and pyro-carbon ( Olander , 2009 ) which are able to contain the radiation .
The coolant used is helium gas instead of water .
Helium gas is superior because it will not get irradiated and does not undergo volume expansion phase change .
Even if the reactor body is breached , there will be no explosive dispersion of radioactive materials .
Therefore , the VHTR design is safer than current nuclear reactors and less susceptible to nuclear arms proliferation ( Hylko , 2008 ) .
Moreover , detractors have argued that the high temperature generated in nuclear reactors is not fully utilized and conventional non-nuclear energy generation methods have better performance in terms of thermal efficiency . Existing nuclear plants have thermal efficiency of approximately 33 % ( Olander , 2009 ) compared to 70 % and above for non-nuclear power plants .
Firstly , the VHTR operates at close to 1000 º C compared to 300-400 º C in current nuclear reactors .
This higher temperature translates to the extraction of 50 % more energy from the same amount of fuel ( Butler , 2004 ) .
Improved engineering and design will further increase the thermal efficiency of the power plant in the future .
Secondly , although the thermal efficiency of nuclear plants is lower , the actual amount of power generated per unit mass of fuel is actually much higher than that of fossil fuels .
Hence the method of using thermal efficiency for comparison is not practical .
Lastly , because of the higher temperature , it is possible to produce hydrogen gas from water thermochemically ( Elder and Allen , 2009 ) .
There is a demand for hydrogen gas which may become a fuel for cars in the future .
Therefore , the VHTR design is superior as it produces more energy and hydrogen gas
Another objection is raised by the taxpayers . They are not convinced with the heavy investment of building a nuclear plant.TS A nuclear plant costs on average , a billion dollars and is designed with a lifespan of sixty years ( Butler , 2004 ) .
Although the price is high , the nuclear plant will not produce any greenhouse or acidic gases .
Acid rain had caused deforestation and affected fisheries . The damages amounted to millions per year .
Global warming has been linked to the more frequent occurrence of natural disasters .
These disasters created uncountable losses in terms of damage to infrastructure , economy and lives .
In rebuttal to the " limited lifespan " of the nuclear plant , in sixty years , this nuclear plant will have produced much cheaper electricity and caused less harm to the environment .
These two advantages are definitely significant .
In conclusion , funding for research on the VHTR must be supported .
This is because the VHTR is much safer due to its superior design and " proliferation-proof " process .
The VHTR will in fact produce more and cheaper energy than existing power plants .
In addition , the VHTR produces useful hydrogen gas and is environmentally friendly .
As such , there is no doubt that this technology is superior .
