Increasing medical cases of genetic disorders are in pace with the development of technology nowadays .
Daily exposure to strong UV radiations by sunlight and chemical substances or carcinogens will definitely have a great impact on human health .
Genetic disorder is considered as one of the severe medicinal consequences due to either those environmental factors or genetic factors by preceding generations .
According to the National Cancer Institute ( n.d. ) in the United States , genetic testing is a DNA analysis done to disclose the increased risk of development of a certain disease or disorder caused by genetic mutation .
Although genetic testing has remarkably improved our life , it still gives rise to many controversial issues , especially ethical concerns .
If one has unfortunately been tested positive in their genetic test , this would more or less place them in an ethical dilemma , whether they should reveal the results to their family members .
Anyhow , as a member of the family , one is obligated to tell the genetic test results to one ' own relatives .
First of all , sharing information and test results with the family can reduce potential disease and even cancer risk among the relatives .
Most hereditary diseases are inherited in autosomal dominant genes which imply that close kin like parents , siblings and children will have 50 percent chances suffering from those diseases ( " Sharing Genetic Test Results " , 2009 ) .
For instance , women from families with hereditary breast cancer should undergo genetic testing or counselling to estimate the expectancy of getting it .
This would help reduce the chance of getting the cancer if preventative measures have been taken earlier .
Moreover , early detection can lead to a lower risk of disease .
Some carriers of genetic diseases object to telling their genetic test results as they think that such disclosure would not help in curing those disorders .
Nonetheless , carriers of genetic diseases are perfectly healthy and their children may only inherit the diseases ( " Genetic Disorders " , 2009 ) .
Thus , revealing genetic test results to relatives is critical to draw their attention on the hereditary diseases .
Subsequently , they might choose to go for genetic tests .
As a result , the chances of suffering from those inherited disorders can be lowered .
At the same time , different curable and controlled treatments can also be carried out for certain diseases , for example , Adenosine deaminase deficiency and asthma .
In addition , positive genetic test results will lead to emotional distress to patients or carriers .
They will undoubtedly fall into the state of severe depression and anxiety .
There is no denying that family is the most intimate and important spiritual pillar to everyone .
Therefore , sharing test results with family might relieve those emotional effects and keep them away from gloomy thoughts .
Some patients and carriers oppose to disclosing the test results as they worry that they will be discriminated at home and experience self-abasement .
However , this might not be true .
In contrast to the problems of discrimination and self-abasement , some people think that disclosing genetic test results can allay grief and obtain more attentive care from close kin during their illness .
Furthermore , grief will only aggravate a patients condition .
Stewart ( 1999 , para .
1 ) stated that the suicide rate after genetic testing for Huntington 's disease was estimated " about 10 times higher than the average population rate " , but it did not tend to be " higher than the overall rate among symptomatic HD carriers " .
Consequently , consolation , support and solace from relatives are very important once the individuals are tested positive for genetic diseases .
Besides that , family members can have proper planning before giving birth if a medical history of hereditary disease in the family is known .
Children may have a 50 percent chance of inheriting the mutated genes from disease-affected parents .
Hence , those couples should avoid having children with devastating diseases , for instance , Down syndrome .
Children suffering from this disease will have slower development physically and mentally
They might also endure other various sicknesses , such as heart defects , Alzheimer 's disease , visual and hearing problems , as well as gastrointestinal blockage .
Genetic disorders like inherited cardiovascular conditions ( ICC ) , which mainly affect the heart , together with blood vessels and cardiac electrical systems , are able to cause immediate and unforeseen death to externally healthy people .
To ensure healthy state of individuals with ICC , proper care , specifically avoidance of triggering events in daily routine , should be delivered by kinfolks ( Burton , H. , Alberg , C. , Stewart , A. , 2009 ) .
All in all , individuals have the right to conceal their genetic test results in order to protect their own privacy .
Federal Privacy Commissioner , Crompton ( 2002 , para .
3 ) pointed out that " the right balance " between willingness of patients and concerns of their relatives has to be taken into consideration as disclosure of health information is rather thorny .
In addition to the importance of sharing genetic test results to reduce disease risk and practise family planning , support but not discrimination by family is the foremost form of assistance to those who are tested positive for genetic diseases during their illness .
Hence , individuals who carry genetic diseases are obligated to disclose their test results among their relatives .
