The human genome project , begun in 1990 , was an ambitious project with the aim of sequencing the 3 billion human DNA and identifying the genes present in these DNA.1 It is no doubt that this project is one of the most successful achievements in the field of biotechnology .
The information gathered enables one to know how the genetic makeup of human correlate to a particular cause of disease.1 However the availability of such tests has brought about other ethical issues , like should the results be announced to the carrier of known genetic risk to his relatives .
I believe this is an issue of personal privacy and rights , the carrier should not have any obligation to inform anyone unless he wishes to do so .
The medical history of an individual is confidential , and prior to revealing the results to anyone , one should seek the permission of the individual and respect his privacy .
One might argue for the fact that the results might affect his immediate family and the carrier has the responsibility of informing them in order for them to take preemptive measures .
Those who took this stand overlook the very fact that genetic testing results only show the presence of the risk . It does not mean that the linked disease will surely manifest itself .
The immediate family might undergo unnecessary stress only to discover that that disorder might not have occurred at all .
Once the results are revealed to the immediate family or relatives , there is no doubt the risk of straining the relationship between all the parties involved .
The individual might risk being discriminated against by his own family and thus the weight of revealing the results should lie solely on the individual .
Many of these genetic tests are for disorder with zero or few known preventive measures.2 In short , it is actually pointless to know the results as there is nothing one could really do about it .
Taking the test and knowing the results might be quite disturbing to the subject involved and might adversely affect his social life . One may take the stand that if the subject knows that he is a carrier , he should tell his spouse about it and together make a careful decision with regard to family planning .
One may also argue that he has a moral obligation now to weight if he should even have children , fearing passing the risk to the next generation .
Those who take this stands are actually discriminating against an individual on a groundless basis , and removing his basic human right to even start and live in a normal family .
Many of such tests only reveal the fact that the subject is at a higher risk of developing the disorder with respect to one without that particular genetic makeup .
The results , however , will not predict when nor whether it will really occur..2 It is ironic that if one were to take a look at the other side of the coin of most of these statistical data data , chances of the particular disorder not occurring within the carriers is actually very much higher .
In conclusion , the decision of informing the subject 's immediate family should lie solely with the subject himself .
He has to assess the pros and cons his of own unique situation and make a choice .
He should not be forced , or under any external influences into revealing the test results . His privacy has to be respected .
One should recognize the fact that these results from genetic testing only point to the fact that one is only at a higher risk of developing the disorder , it does not mean the disorder will surely occur
