Rapid development in genetic field has made genetic testing a potential method to be used in healthcare improvement .
Genetic testing is carried out mainly for diagnosis confirmation , prediction of heritable-disorder diseases and identification of carrier carrier of disorder without physical symptom ( National Human Genome Institute , 2008 ) .
Sharing of test positive results with relatives is necessary as it is the relatives ' right to know about it .
It also alerts them to early detection and treatment .
Indirectly , it could help in medical research development and make treatment for currently untreatable diseases possible .
Hence , a carrier of a known genetic risk is obligated to tell his or her relatives .
Firstly , genetic information could be extremely personal to an individual ( Canadian Cystic Fibrosis foundation , 2000 ) .
Confidentiality of such information should be protected so that one 's privacy could be respected .
However , genetic information comes in two kinds .
The first one is the mutation in a family while the latter is one 's own genetic status .
( Skene , n.d. ) .
It is alright for not sharing own 's genetic status but sharing of mutation in family with blood relatives is essential as it affects the blood relatives as well .
They have the right to know about it .
Moreover , best clinical practice requires family history to assess the usefulness of genetic testing ( Parker and Lucassen , n.d. ) .
Hence , there is no reason why one could benefit from family history but omit relatives ' rights to have such benefits as well .
Genetic discrimination by health insurers and employers could be one of the main hindrances that stop people from sharing their genetic information with blood relatives ( National Human Genome Institute , 2008 ) .
This would be protected once President Bush signed the Genetic Information Nondiscrimination Act of 2008 ( The White House , 2008 ) .
Besides that , some types of cancer such as breast and ovarian cancer seem to run in the family mainly due to an inherited altered gene ( National Cancer Institute , n.d. ) Examples of such genes could be BRCA1 and BRCA2 ( Breast Cancer Gene 1 and Breast Cancer Gene 2 ) which could pass down from father or mother .
Revealing of positive test results to relatives allows them to go for early detection and treatment which can possibly save lives .
Limitations of genetic testing could be the lack of treatment strategies for certain disorders once diagnosed
( Genetics Home Reference ) .
It may be argued that since there is no treatment , there is no point of revealing to relatives even though it shows positive result .
But , a genetic test that identifies a carrier of a certain disease could help couples to decide whether they would like to bear children and weigh the risk of passing down an altered gene
( Barett ) .
Lastly , revealing positive test results might increase the number of people going for genetic testing .
This indirectly increases the possibility of people giving consent to take part in research for genetic related diseases .
With such , improved or new treatment is possible for genetic related diseases and could possibly benefit the carrier or even his or her family members .
In conclusion , revealing genetic information to the relatives does not seem to be of great disadvantage to an individual but could be of great advantage to blood relatives .
Besides the right to know , most important is the possibility of early detection and treatment for blood relatives .
Development of new treatment for currently untreatable diseases is also possible .
Therefore , a carrier of a known genetic risk is obligated to tell his or her relatives .
