The individual requesting genetic testing should have the final say in whether to reveal the results of such testing
As genetic testing becomes more and more popular , the moral issues around it have also been revealed in face of the mass public .
Genetic testing is the newest technology aimed to " test for genetic disorders " , with " direct examination of the DNA molecule " also included ( Human Genome Program , 2008 ) .
The wide spread of this technology has sparked an even wider range of heated discussion on who has the final say with regard to the testing result .
Since the individual who undergoes the testing is the direct victim from this examination and most related to the result , he or she should have the right to decide whether to reveal the result or not .
What to emphasize is that the choice includes both possibilities of hiding and revealing the findings .
The individual is privileged to prefer the most appreciate approach based on his or her knowledge and the surroundings .
To authorize the individual to determine is one specific aspect of the protection of human rights .
First of all , one 's private life is a main part of human rights .
According to the definition of private life provided by the European Convention on Human Rights , it " includes the right to be protected from the unwanted publication or disclosure of intimate personal information " CITATION Eth06 \ l 1033 ( Ethics in genetic research and practice , 2006 ) .
As mentioned before , the individual undergoing the genetic test is one who is most directly affected by this genetic testing , so he or she should decide whether to tell anyone else .
Imagine that the top-secret information is released regardless of how the individual feels .
The rule of protecting human rights is violated .
Thus the individual should have the final say .
Secondly , the result might be misused against the individual if the choice is left to other parties .
Once the information that an otherwise normal employee is likely to be diagnosed with some rare gene-related diseases , the employer might be reluctant to keep him or her because the employer knows that the duration of the employee 's commitment may be disturbed by sudden illnesses .
Possible breakups might take place before marriage simply due to the fiance 's comparatively higher chance to pass the so-called " bad " gene to the next generation .
Hence the individual who takes the genetic test should be the one to decide how to deal with the result .
Opponents may argue that the individual 's family also have their human rights , that is , the rights to know the result of the genetic test .
It can not be denied that they also have the right to know if their genes also possess lurking variables that can cause diseases .
The issue in question now is not whether the individual has the final say but rather whether the individual can hide the truth from others .
A normal person will probably choose the best solution for his or her family .
The cases of hiding the result because of selfishness can be decreased by improving the education of moral values .
If the decision is left to others , the chance of perfect equality is also questionable .
What is more , people may be reluctant to undergo testing if they can not decide on their own .
Then the purpose of this advanced technology can not be realized .
To conclude , the individual requesting genetic testing should have the final say in whether to tell the result or not .
Reference :
