Turning Into a Surveillance Society ?
} [ HYPERLINK : http : //www.aclu.org/privacy/gen/index.html ] " ( in English ) .
American Civil Liberties Union .
Technological advancement has led to the invention of several surveillance technologies .
One such example is Radio Frequency Identification ( RFID ) technology .
The use of such technology has made it possible to track people and their activities through their belongings without their knowledge .
This has brought about many debates on the ethical and moral issues surrounding the use of the technology ( Anonymous , 2005 ) .
While proponents of RFID suggest that the use of RFID brings benefits such as convenience and protection to patients and children , others view it as a form of security threat and intrusion of privacy ( Data Privacy & Integrity Advisory Committee , 2006 ) .
The use of RFID can also cause the growth of tumors if chips are implanted into the human body ( Anti-chip.com , n.d ) .
Thus , the use of RFID should be banned as the consequences it brings outweigh the few benefits that it provides .
Firstly , the use of RFID to track people has raised concerns on privacy issues as they are tracked without their knowledge ( Data Privacy & Integrity Advisory Committee , 2006 ) .
Their whereabouts and activities can be constantly monitored and such information shared .
It can be argued that the use of RFID tags to check on patients with Alzheimer conditions and children may be a form of protection .
For instance , in 2006 , Verichip , the manufacturer of RFID chips for humans attempted to chip 200 Alzheimer patients from Chattanooga , Tennessee to test the effectiveness of the product ( Antichip.com , n.d ) .
However , this raised a series of heated debates as it is not ethical to chip cognitively impaired individuals who are unable to give informed consent for the experiment ( Antichip.com , n.d ) .
Hence , the use of RFID should be banned as it invades the privacy of people .
Secondly , the use of RFID to track people may provide a false sense of security ( Hook , 2005 ) .
RFID tags can be incorporated into one 's belongings such as passports and identification cards to track and identify people .
This may increase cases of skimming on personal information .
Supporters of RFID may argue that encryption can be used to limit the access of information to unauthorized people ( Data Privacy & Integrity Advisory Committee , 2006 ) .
Furthermore , it can be argued that RFID technology can reduce delays at checkpoints and entrances which in turn would improve efficiency and speed ( Data Privacy & Integrity Advisory Committee , 2006 ) .
Nevertheless , experiments by security research firm Riscure have shown that the encrypted passport can be intercepted and excessive information can be revealed to an unauthorized person , which may promote identity theft ( Hasbrouck , 2006 ) .
Therefore , RFID should be banned as the technology is flawed .
Lastly , implanting RFID chips in humans can be a potential health risk .
A series of studies showed that chip implants induced malignant tumors in some lab mice and rats ( Lewan , 2007 ) .
This shows that implanting chips in humans may cause cancer .
However , leading cancer specialists reviewed that animal test results do not necessarily apply to humans ( Lewan , 2007 ) .
Moreover , Verichip Corp insists that the chips are safe and has been implanted in millions of domestic pets without major problems ( Lewan , 2007 ) .
Nevertheless , implanting RFID in the human body should be banned before any cause of detrimental health effects on individuals .
In conclusion , the use of RFID to track people should be banned as it can be a security threat and it intrudes on the privacy of people .
Implanting RFID chips into humans may also result in cancer .
The disadvantages of the use of RFID outweigh the benefits .
