The issue of whether to use radio-frequency identification ( RFID ) to track people has been highly debated ever since it was created .
It may appear that RFID and other surveillance technology have brought benefits to public security and the efficiency to attend to a patient .
In fact , the use of RFID and other surveillance technology has raised issues such as our basic privacy , health and the public security .
Therefore , I totally agree that surveillance technology should not be used to track people .
Firstly , surveillance technology such as the use of RFID should be banned as it intrudes upon our privacy .
According to the Universal Declaration of Human Rights , Article 12 , it says that 'no one shall be subjected to arbitrary interference with his privacy , family , home or correspondence , nor to attacks upon his honour and reputation ' ( United Nations , 1948 ) .
Using surveillance technology to track people violates their privacy ; thus , it is a very serious offence .
Furthermore , a study has revealed that it is very easy to hack into devices such as RFID cards in passports .
According to the experiment conducted by Researcher Chris Paget who is a Security Consultant at NGS Software Ltd , by using simple gadgets such as Matrics antenna and Motorola reader , we will be able to retrieve personal data from the U.S. passport cards ( sixwise.com , 2009 ) .
Opposition may argue that now passport has a protective RFID-blocking sleeve to prevent intrusion from hackers ( sixwise.com , 2009 ) .
However , as technology advances to prevent such intrusions , hackers and stalkers can advance to break into such protections too .
This means that the personal data of a person can be easily retrieved and thus one 's privacy is invaded .
Therefore , surveillance technology such as RFID should not be used as it violates the human rights for privacy .
Secondly , the use of RFID can cause serious health hazard .
RFID is used in hospitals to keep track on the condition of patients .
It indeed has brought great convenience and efficiency in attending to the needs of patients .
However , the use of RFID not only poses a threat to the patient , but to everyone around it .
Studies showed that when active RFID tags were shifted around 41 important health devices such as pacemakers , they interfered with the devices in 34 tests out of all 123 tests .
And out of the 34 tests , 22 could cause a serious problem to the patients ( Wasserman , 2008 ) .
Despite the convenience of being able to attend to the patients efficiently , the real problem will be that RFID can be interfered by another RFID as well .
This will cause a serious problem .
Furthermore , studies have shown that when RFID is implanted into mice , it causes the growth of tumour around RFID ( Wasserman , 2008 ) . RFID not only poses a risk to patients , but intensive exposure can be hazardous to a healthy individual too .
Hence , RFID can be a health hazard ; thus , it should not be used .
Lastly , surveillance technology like CCTV cameras does not decrease the crime rate .
CCTV cameras are used to " keep an eye " on the public and are said to be a good way of crime prevention .
Opposition argues that with CCTV cameras , criminals will be aware that they are being watched and so the crime rate is reduced .
However , this is not necessarily true .
London has more than ten thousand CCTV cameras ; however , eighty percent of the crimes are left unsolved ( Davenport , 2007 ) .
This substantially supports that surveillance technology does not reduce the crime rate and enhance public security .
Hence , it is not necessary to use surveillance technology to track people .
To conclude , surveillance technology should not be used as it not only can not help solve crimes and maintain order , it also poses a health threat to the people using it and around it as well .
Finally , the use of surveillance technology is a violation to human rights .
Therefore I agree that it should not be used .
