The use of surveillance technology such as RFID to track people should be banned
It is not a dream for your parents to know where you are and what you are doing any more , even if they are both miles away from you .
In the 21st century , the superman is not the only one who has the power to privies what is in your bag without asking you , but ordinary people can also have the power to do so. .
What they rely on is not a magic mirror but a kind of surveillance technology called RFID ( radio frequency identification ) .
By implanting a chip to human body or attaching a tag to a product , the information of a certain person or an object can be scanned easily by a scanner .
However , does a child really want his or her parents to track him or her all the time ?
At the same time , will you feel comfortable when everyone can have the access to know what you are taking with you ?
The convenience and problems this technology brings to us make it hard for us to decide whether it should be legal to track people by using the technology .
In this case it can not be denied that RFID is a beneficial technology , while it is an " expensive and intrusive way to track people " , because of three main reasons ( Schatz , 2006 ) .
Firstly , the RFID technology is still immature , which means we can not guarantee both the accuracy of information we get and the safety of these chips implanted in our body .
Actually , when two or more readers want to scan the same tag , the " signals from their scanners may overlap " which will leave the tag " unable to reply " ( Reported on Techonovelgy.com ) .
We call this reader collision .
Similarly , " tag collision " may occur when " tags are present in a small area " ( Reported on Techonovelgy.com ) .
Furthermore , the RFID chips work by using electromagnetic waves , which may have some potential harm to our bodies .
However , people may argue that the chips and tags will be tested before using .
As another evidence for this technology 's immaturity , there is no " standard " ( Campbell , 2004 ) in this area .
As a result , the organizations which conduct these tests are the RFID companies themselves .
Thus their accuracy and safety declarations are usually not objective .
Secondly , tracking people may result in privacy crisis , which can cause more serious problems .
When you are implanted with a RFID chip or when you take some objects with RFID tags , the information about your location , your purchase list and so forth can be easily disclosed .
You can never have a leisurely afternoon , because your fellows can always find you during your vocation .
You can never keep your diet a secret , since your neighbors can easily know what you buy for food today .
The RFID supporters may argue that a lot of protection is provided in this case .
However , they should take note that electromagnetic waves are neither some entity which can be locked in a safe , nor digital data which can be stored in a computer .
" Coding " or " adding pin numbers " to them can not stop people from getting them ( Reported on Technovelgy.com ) .
Thirdly , it is purposeless to track everyone .
It is true that RFID is really helpful for finding lost children or tracing thefts .
However , only a very small percentage of people will get lost .
Similarly , not so many products will be stolen .
It will be meaningless and costly to track everyone just in case some of them may get lost someday .
Based on the three reasons above , RFID is not reliable enough to give correct information about the tracked person .
Meanwhile , it may invade users ' privacy .
Finally , it is costly as well as unnecessary to track people .
Thus , it should be clear that tracking people by RFID should be banned .
